  350:   `= 1` to `= 2`, **and** gains two explicit named checks: exactly one
  351:   `iolbox-server-<v>.tar.gz` and exactly one
  352:   `iolbox-server-<v>-linux-arm64.tar.gz`. A bare count of 2 would pass on two
  353:   arm64 payloads.
  354: - Both payloads' digests are checked against their trusted `--payload*-sha256`.
  355: - The reproducibility re-pack loop passes both pairs through to the packer.
  356: - The forbidden-member regex is unchanged. Verified by inspection that
  357:   `iolbox-server-<v>-linux-arm64.tar.gz` does not match any alternative in
  358:   `(\._[^/]*|\.DS_Store|.*namedfork.*|.*resourcefork.*|.*cisco.*|.*\.i86bi[^/]*|.*\.iol[^/]*)$`
  359:   ƒ?" in particular `.*\.iol[^/]*$` needs a literal `.iol`, and the archive path
  360:   contains `/iolbox`, not `.iolbox`.
  361: 
  362: **New assertion worth adding:** the arm64 payload's inner `manifest.env` says
  363: `arch=arm64`. The layout test should extract it and assert that, so a mislabelled
  364: payload is caught by the packaging gate rather than 40 minutes into a guest
  365: provision. Cheap, and it is the only in-archive proof that the second payload
  366: is genuinely arm64.
  367: 
  368: ---
  369: 
  370: ## 7. Launcher changes (`tools/iolab-launcher`)
  371: 
  372: ### 7.1 Signature
  373: 
  374: ```go
  375: func selectPayload(explicit, assetRoot, profileName string) (string, error)
  376: ```
  377: 
  378: Callers: `macos_cli.go:258` passes `selection.ProfileName`.
  379: 
  380: ### 7.2 Matching rules
  381: 
  382: Let `wantARM64 := profileName == nativeProfileTableName`.
  383: 
  384: - **`explicit != ""` (i.e. `IOLBOX_TARBALL` / `--tarball`) still wins
  385:   unconditionally.** It keeps its exact current behaviour: stat, must be a
  386:   regular file, return it. This is non-negotiable for backward compatibility ƒ?"
  387:   every M4ƒ?"M7 hardware harness in `packaging/macos/tests/` drives the launcher
  388:   by pointing `IOLBOX_TARBALL` at a hand-built tarball with an arbitrary path,
  389:   and hard-failing on an arch/name mismatch would break all of them.
  390:   We do add a **warning** (not an error) to stderr when the explicit
  391:   basename's arch tag contradicts the selected profile, so a human staging the
  392:   wrong file by hand sees it immediately.
  393: - **`wantARM64`**: candidate must have prefix `iolbox-server-` and suffix
  394:   `-linux-arm64.tar.gz`.
  395: - **otherwise (amd64/Rosetta)**: candidate must have prefix `iolbox-server-`
  396:   and suffix `.tar.gz`, and must **not** have suffix `-linux-arm64.tar.gz`.
  397:   `iolbox-server-<v>-linux-amd64.tar.gz` is accepted, because
  398:   `pack-native.sh --arch amd64` can legitimately produce it even though CI
  399:   does not.
  400: - Sorting (newest mtime, lexicographic tie-break) and the two search roots
  401:   (`assetRoot`, then `cwd` if different) are unchanged. Once the candidate set
  402:   is arch-filtered the tie-break is no longer load-bearing, but leaving it
  403:   alone keeps the diff minimal and keeps behaviour identical for the
  404:   single-payload case.
  405: - Error text names the arch and the profile:
  406:   `no iolbox-server-*-linux-arm64.tar.gz payload found for profile "native-arm64"`.
  407: 
  408: ### 7.3 Why key on `nativeProfileTableName` and not the `NATIVE` role
  409: 
  410: The role column (`NATIVE`) is arguably more semantic, but every other
  411: arch-sensitive decision in the launcher keys on the
  412: `nativeProfileTableName` constant (`resolveProfileSelectionName`,
  413: `nativePreflight`). Using a second, different key for the same concept invites
  414: them to disagree later. One key. Noted as a deliberate choice, not an
  415: oversight.
  416: 
  417: ### 7.4 Backward compatibility, enumerated
  418: 
  419: | Situation | Behaviour |
  420: |---|---|
  421: | Old archive (1 untagged payload), new launcher, `auto` | native preflight fails on the missing pin file ƒ+' `auto-fallback-rosetta` ƒ+' amd64 matcher finds the untagged payload. **Works, unchanged.** |
  422: | New archive, `auto`, non-qualifying host | `auto-fallback-rosetta` ƒ+' amd64 matcher ƒ+' untagged payload. **Works.** |
  423: | New archive, `auto`, qualifying host | `auto-native` ƒ+' arm64 matcher ƒ+' tagged payload. **New behaviour, intended.** |
  424: | New archive, `--profile rosetta-amd64` | amd64 matcher ƒ+' untagged payload. **Works.** |
  425: | New archive, `IOLBOX_TARBALL` set | explicit wins, as today, + a warning on mismatch. **Works.** |
  426: | User extracted new archive over an old one | arch filtering makes this strictly *better* than today's mtime lottery. |
  427: 
  428: ### 7.5 Out of scope, flagged not skipped
  429: 
  430: - **`stageFiles` hardcodes `30-canary.sh`.** `macos_lima.go` asserts
  431:   `test -f $newDir/30-canary.sh` regardless of profile, never using
  432:   `p.canaryStep()`. It passes today only because the whole `guest/` directory
  433:   is copied and `30-canary.sh` happens to be present ƒ?" which remains true
  434:   after this change, since both canaries ship. So this is latent, not
  435:   triggered, and fixing it is a behaviour change to the Rosetta path's
  436:   staging assertions. **Flagged, not fixed here.**
  437: - **`packaging/macos/iolbox-mac.sh`** is the Rosetta-era shell harness. It is
  438:   explicitly excluded from the archive (the layout test's forbidden-member
  439:   regex names it), its step list hardcodes `40-install-payload.sh`, and its
  440:   `discover_payload()` has the same un-filtered glob. Pointed at a
  441:   two-payload directory it would pick by mtime. It has **no native profile
  442:   support at all** and giving it some is a separate piece of work.
  443:   **Flagged, not fixed here.**
  444: - **`payload_version` cosmetics.** `40-install-payload-native.sh` derives
  445:   `payload_version="${payload_name#iolbox-server-}"; ...%.tar.gz`, so an arm64
  446:   payload logs `v0.5.3-linux-arm64` as its "version". Cosmetic, in one log
  447:   line. Left alone deliberately ƒ?" trimming it would mean editing a guest
  448:   script that the M7 hardware evidence was collected against.
  449: 
  450: ---
  451: 
  452: ## 8. Release notes and `THIRD_PARTY.md`
  453: 
  454: ### 8.1 Release-notes text in `release.yml`
  455: 
  456: The current sentence is now wrong:
  457: 
  458: > The existing amd64 supervisor/VPCS and **x86_64 IOL only** run through
  459: > Rosetta; i386/i86bi and arm64-native IOL are unsupported.
  460: 
  461: Replacement must say, accurately:
  462: 
  463: - The archive carries **two** payloads; the launcher picks one per resolved
  464:   profile.
  465: - `native-arm64` runs the supervisor, VPCS, and tool packs as **real arm64
  466:   binaries**; x86_64 IOL is still translated, by **qemu-user inside the
  467:   guest** rather than by Rosetta.
  468: - The Rosetta profiles (`debian13` default, `jammy` compatibility) are
  469:   unchanged and run the amd64 payload under Rosetta.
  470: - **x86_64 IOL only, on both profiles.** i386/i86bi remain unsupported, and
  471:   arm64-native IOL remains unsupported (nobody ships an aarch64 IOL).
  472: - `auto` prefers native on a qualifying host and **falls back to Rosetta**
  473:   when the native preflight fails; `--profile native-arm64` fails closed;
  474:   `IOLBOX_PROFILE=rosetta-amd64` is the durable opt-out.
  475: - `native-arm64` has **no row in `IOLBOX_QUALIFICATION_TABLE`**, so the
  476:   launcher prints `UNMEASURED ƒ?" CANARY REQUIRED` for it. The notes should not
  477:   imply a qualified profile. This is honest and matches A0.3.
  478: 
  479: ### 8.2 `THIRD_PARTY.md` ƒ?" yes, it must change, and it ships
  480: 
  481: `THIRD_PARTY.md` is manifest entry 3: it is copied into the archive as
  482: `notices/THIRD_PARTY.md`. It is a **shipped legal notice**, and it currently
  483: contains statements that this change falsifies:
  484: 
  485: 1. "the exact native Linux payload produced by `runtime/pack-native.sh`" ƒ?"
  486:    singular. Now two.
  487: 2. "builds its Linux/amd64 binary; `runtime/pack-native.sh` places that binary
  488:    at `bin/vpcs`" ƒ?" now built twice, amd64 and arm64, from the same pinned
  489:    `v0.8.3` / `3870ae8` ref.
  490: 3. "x86_64 IOL is the only supported IOL architecture in this Rosetta
  491:    profile" ƒ?" the archive is no longer single-profile.
  492: 4. "QEMU is **not** in the Apple Silicon archive. The QEMU notice below
  493:    applies only to the separate Windows launcher bundle and must not be read
  494:    as a claim that the Mac artifact contains QEMU."
  495: 
  496: Point 4 is the delicate one. The literal claim **remains true** ƒ?" no QEMU
  497: binary is in the tarball. But `packaging/macos/guest/10-multiarch-native.sh`
  498: runs, in the guest:
  499: 
  500: ```sh
  501: DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends \
  502:     qemu-user-static binfmt-support libc6:amd64 libssl3t64:amd64
  503: ```
  504: 
  505: so choosing the native profile *causes* GPL'd QEMU to be installed on the
  506: user's machine. Leaving a notice that reads "must not be read as a claim that
  507: the Mac artifact contains QEMU" without qualifying it would be misleading by
  508: omission once native is the `auto` default.
  509: 
  510: **My legal analysis** (stated so the owner can overrule it, not asserted as
  511: settled): iolbox does not redistribute qemu-user-static. Debian does, from
  512: Debian's own repositories, to the user's own guest, under Debian's own
  513: copyright files and source offer. GPL-2.0's source-provision obligation
  514: attaches to the distributor of the binary; iolbox is not that party, exactly
  515: as it is not the distributor of the Debian guest image, the apt indexes, or
  516: any other guest package ƒ?" a boundary this notice **already** draws for the
  517: guest image. So the obligation I believe is real here is **accuracy of
  518: notice**, and that is what I propose to discharge: rewrite the paragraph to
  519: say plainly that the native profile installs `qemu-user-static` and
  520: `binfmt-support` into the guest from Debian at provisioning time, that they
  521: are GPL-licensed, that iolbox does not redistribute them, and where Debian's
  522: corresponding source lives.
  523: 
  524: ### 8.3 P7-02 ƒ?" surfaced, not silently discharged
  525: 
  526: `docs/macos-m7-result.md` row **P7-02** is `UNEVALUATED`. Its raw item
  527: (`.../qemu-user/selection/translator-selection.log`) records
  528: `qemu-user-static 1:10.0.11+ds-0+deb13u1`, `binfmt-support 2.2.2-7+b1`, both
  529: copyright sha256s, `owner_image_excluded: true`,
  530: `owner_licence_excluded: true`, `source_notice_obligations_recorded: true` ƒ?"
  531: **and `redistribution_review_required: true`, with no record anywhere that
  532: the review was performed.**
  533: 
  534: I am **not** marking that review done. Writing an accurate notice is not the
  535: same act as performing the redistribution review the M7 contract demanded, and
  536: I have no authority to record one. Position:
  537: 
  538: > **Prerequisite, owner action required.** Fixing the notice (A8.2) is in
  539: > scope and is done here because shipping a knowingly misleading legal notice
  540: > is worse than the status quo. Discharging `redistribution_review_required:
  541: > true` is **not** in scope and remains open. It should be closed before the
  542: > first tag that ships the native profile, and my A8.2 analysis is offered as
  543: > input to it, not as its conclusion.
  544: 
  545: This is a blocking item for *tagging*, not for merging this branch.
  546: 
  547: ### 8.4 `packaging/macos/README.release.md`
  548: 
  549: Also ships (as `README.md`). Its prerequisite list says "Apple Silicon macOS
  550: with **Rosetta available**", which is wrong for the native profile, and its
  551: "Upgrade and support boundary" says the runtime executes everything "under
  552: Rosetta". Both need the same two-profile treatment as A8.1. In scope.
  553: 
  554: ---
  555: 
  556: ## 9. Rollout and versioning
  557: 
  558: **Decision: a normal next tag. No new feature flag, no opt-in period.**
  559: 
  560: Arguments for:
  561: 
  562: - The opt-in/opt-out mechanism **already exists and is richer than a flag
  563:   would be**: `--profile` / `IOLBOX_PROFILE`, a persisted choice in
  564:   `~/Library/Application Support/iolbox/profile-choice.env`, and `auto`'s
  565:   fail-open-to-Rosetta. Adding a build-time flag on top would be a fourth,
  566:   redundant control surface.
  567: - `auto` already prefers native in shipped code (`e2ffe34`, owner ruling). A
  568:   flag here would *contradict* a decision the owner already made rather than
  569:   gate a new one.
  570: - Every native failure path degrades to Rosetta except forced
  571:   `--profile native-arm64`, which is an explicit user request to fail closed.
  572: 
  573: But the honest counterweight, which the release notes must carry:
  574: 
  575: - **This change is what makes `auto`'s native preference actually bite.**
  576:   Today the preference is inert: the pin file is missing from every shipped
  577:   archive, so `auto` always falls back. After this, `auto` will genuinely land
  578:   on native for qualifying hosts. Users upgrading in place will change
  579:   runtime, guest, and translator without asking for it.
  580: - The gate ledger is `NO-PROMOTE` with 29 UNEVALUATED rows (A0.3), and
  581:   P7R-02 records the **Rosetta** four-node tier as a hard `FAIL` 0/2 while
  582:   native measured 4/4 ƒ?" the two arms genuinely diverge and neither is fully
  583:   characterised.
  584: 
  585: **Therefore:** normal tag, but the first tag carrying this ships with an
  586: explicit, prominent notes line naming native-arm64 as the new default on
  587: qualifying Apple Silicon and naming `IOLBOX_PROFILE=rosetta-amd64` as the
  588: one-line opt-out. That is a documentation-level rollout control, which is the
  589: right weight for a change whose escape hatches already exist and are tested.
  590: A code-level flag would be over-engineering a blocker.
  591: 
  592: ---
  593: 
  594: ## 10. Local verification plan, and its honest limits
  595: 
  596: No GitHub Actions run is available. Simulation on the Windows workstation:
  597: 
  598: | Step | Feasible locally? |
  599: |---|---|
  600: | `build-release.sh --arch amd64,arm64` | **Yes** ƒ?" pure Go cross-compile + one Vite build. Baseline already run green. |
  601: | `pack-native.sh --arch arm64` toollaunch + 7 tool packs + secbench attacks | **Yes** ƒ?" all Go, `CGO_ENABLED=0`. |
  602: | `fetch-vpcs.sh --arch arm64` | **No.** No `gcc`, no `aarch64-linux-gnu-gcc`, no WSL distro, no Docker on this box. |
  603: | `pack-release.sh` with both payloads | **Yes** ƒ?" GNU tar 1.35, `file`, `sha256sum`, `gzip` all present. |
  604: | `release-layout-test.sh` | **Yes.** |
  605: | `go build/vet/test ./...` in `tools/iolab-launcher` | **Yes.** |
  606: | `release.yml` YAML parse | **Yes** (`python3 -c "import yaml; ..."`). |
  607: | Actually running the native guest on a Mac | **No.** Out of reach here. |
  608: 
  609: For the one gap, the arm64 VPCS binary, I will substitute a **clearly
  610: labelled `linux/arm64` Go stand-in** passed via `--vpcs-bin`. It is a genuine
  611: aarch64 ELF, so it exercises `require_elf_arch` and every packaging assertion
  612: truthfully ƒ?" but the resulting tarball is a **plumbing fixture, not a
  613: releasable payload**, and will be described that way in the report and never
  614: committed. The real `aarch64-linux-gnu-gcc` static link of VPCS is the single
  615: step in this plan that first executes on CI, and that is stated plainly rather
  616: than papered over.
