    1: # iolbox macOS/Lima profile data. Both tables are pipe-delimited so
    2: # qualification is an exact lookup, never a version comparison.
    3: #
    4: # Static profile table columns (12, Phase 4 onward):
    5: # name|role|guest_label|pin_env|yaml_template|multiarch_step|kernel_hold_step|kernel_series|expected_uname_r|canary_step|install_step|verify_step
    6: #
    7: # The trailing three columns are Phase-4-era per-profile guest step
    8: # overrides. Every Rosetta-era row keeps the historical fixed filenames
    9: # explicitly for clarity, even though macos_profiles.go's canaryStep()/
   10: # installStep()/verifyStep() would default to the same names if these three
   11: # columns were left empty (parseProfilesEnv also still accepts the old
   12: # 9-field row shape for any external caller that never heard of Phase 4).
   13: #
   14: # native-arm64 is Phase 4's own row: a real arm64 supervisor/vpcs/toollaunch
   15: # build (runtime/pack-native.sh --arch arm64), Rosetta disabled at the VM
   16: # level (packaging/macos/lima/iolbox-native-arm64.yaml), and its own
   17: # canary/install/verify scripts that fail closed if a Rosetta binfmt entry
   18: # is ever found registered in this guest. See docs/m7-phase4-file-mapping.md
   19: # for why this row could not be added until those scripts existed.
   20: IOLBOX_PROFILE_TABLE='debian13|DEFAULT|Debian 13 trixie|pinned-image-debian13.env|iolbox-trixie.yaml|10-multiarch-debian.sh|20-kernel-hold-debian.sh|6.12|6.12.101+deb13-cloud-arm64|30-canary.sh|40-install-payload.sh|50-verify.sh
   21: jammy|COMPATIBILITY|Ubuntu 22.04|pinned-image.env|iolbox-jammy.yaml|10-multiarch.sh|20-kernel-hold.sh|5.15||30-canary.sh|40-install-payload.sh|50-verify.sh
   22: debian12|CANDIDATE|Debian 12 bookworm|pinned-image-debian12.env|iolbox-bookworm.yaml|10-multiarch-debian.sh|20-kernel-hold-debian.sh|6.1||30-canary.sh|40-install-payload.sh|50-verify.sh
   23: native-arm64|NATIVE|Debian 13 trixie (native arm64)|pinned-image-native-arm64.env|iolbox-native-arm64.yaml|10-multiarch-native.sh|20-kernel-hold-debian.sh|6.12|6.12.101+deb13-cloud-arm64|30-canary-native.sh|40-install-payload-native.sh|50-verify-native.sh'
   24: 
   25: # Exact-host qualification table columns:
   26: # profile|macos_product|macos_build|verdict|status|evidence
   27: IOLBOX_QUALIFICATION_TABLE='jammy|13.5|22G74|PASS|SUPPORTED|docs/macos-m0-result.md
   28: jammy|26.6.1|25G76|PASS|SUPPORTED|docs/macos-m1-result.md 2026-08-14
   29: debian13|26.6.1|25G76|PASS|SUPPORTED|docs/macos-m1-result.md 2026-08-14 full E2E: canary PASS, v0.5.2 installed, two-node IOL 90% (9/10), restart persistence'
===== packaging/macos/README.release.md =====
    1: # iolbox on Apple Silicon macOS
    2: 
    3: This unsigned archive is the Apple Silicon (darwin/arm64) iolbox launcher,
    4: the exact Linux payload produced by the same release workflow, and the locked
    5: Lima profiles/provisioner it needs. It contains no guest disk, hypervisor,
    6: Lima installation, or Cisco software. You supply a legally held **x86_64 IOL**
    7: image; i386/i86bi and arm64-native IOL are unsupported.
    8: 
    9: ## Quick start
   10: 
   11: Prerequisites:
   12: 
   13: - Apple Silicon macOS with Rosetta available;
   14: - Lima installed and managed separately by you (Apache-2.0; it is not
   15:   redistributed or installed, upgraded, or removed by iolbox).
   16: 
   17: Download iolbox-macos-arm64.tar.gz and its matching
   18: iolbox-macos-arm64.tar.gz.sha256 from the same release. Verify the outer
   19: checksum before extracting:
   20: 
   21: ~~~sh
   22: shasum -a 256 -c iolbox-macos-arm64.tar.gz.sha256
   23: mkdir extract
   24: tar -xzf iolbox-macos-arm64.tar.gz -C extract
   25: cd extract/iolbox-macos-arm64
   26: shasum -a 256 -c SHA256SUMS
   27: ~~~
   28: 
   29: The artifact is unsigned. Before the first run, inspect quarantine on the
   30: downloaded archive, extracted directory, and launcher:
   31: 
   32: ~~~sh
   33: xattr -l /path/to/iolbox-macos-arm64.tar.gz || true
   34: xattr -p com.apple.quarantine /path/to/iolbox-macos-arm64.tar.gz; echo "archive xattr exit=$?"
   35: xattr -l .; echo "directory xattr exit=$?"
   36: xattr -p com.apple.quarantine ./iolbox; echo "binary xattr exit=$?"
   37: ~~~
   38: 
   39: If the launcher has com.apple.quarantine, remove that attribute from the
   40: launcher only, after the checksum checks above:
   41: 
   42: ~~~sh
   43: xattr -d com.apple.quarantine ./iolbox
   44: ~~~
   45: 
   46: Then start the product from the extracted directory with one command:
   47: 
   48: ~~~sh
   49: ./iolbox start
   50: ~~~
   51: 
   52: The launcher creates or reuses the durable named Lima guest
   53: iolbox-debian13, verifies the VZ/Rosetta canary and pinned Debian 13/trixie
   54: guest/kernel (6.12.101+deb13-cloud-arm64), and serves the GUI on
   55: http://127.0.0.1:4001. The GUI, console, and capture forwards are
   56: loopback-only and have no authentication.
   57: 
   58: For the compatibility profile, select jammy; it is pinned to Ubuntu 22.04
   59: with the qualified 5.15 guest/kernel line. The Debian 12/bookworm files are
   60: shipped as an unqualified candidate and are refused while their digest remains
   61: unpinned.
   62: 
   63: Host data defaults to:
   64: 
   65: ~~~text
   66: ~/Library/Application Support/iolbox/images
   67: ~/Library/Application Support/iolbox/labs
   68: ~~~
   69: 
   70: ./iolbox stop stops the guest and preserves its VM, images, labs, license,
   71: and Lima cache. Removing the extracted launcher directory is ordinary,
   72: non-destructive uninstall. An optional destructive reset may delete only the
   73: named iolbox-debian13 Lima machine and move the two iolbox data paths to
   74: Trash after explicit checks; it is not part of ordinary launcher removal.
   75: 
   76: ## Upgrade and support boundary
   77: 
   78: From a later extracted release, run ./iolbox upgrade in the existing data
   79: environment. It preserves host images/labs, the host identity and license,
   80: the Lima machine, and the pinned guest kernel while replacing the guest
   81: payload.
   82: 
   83: The runtime executes the existing Linux supervisor/VPCS and x86_64 IOL under
   84: Rosetta inside the arm64 Lima guest. This release supports **x86_64 IOL only**;
   85: i386/i86bi and arm64-native IOL are not supported and are not made runnable by
   86: the archive.
   87: 
   88: The installation guide for this exact release is:
   89: <https://github.com/rohan-punj/iolbox/blob/@VERSION@/docs/INSTALL.md>
   90: 
   91: The archive's SHA-256 files detect corruption or a changed download. Because
   92: this release is unsigned, they do not authenticate the publisher. Lima fetches
   93: the digest-locked Debian/Ubuntu guest image and apt packages at provisioning
   94: time; those guest assets are not embedded here. See notices/THIRD_PARTY.md
   95: for the distributed VPCS notice and the boundary around Windows-only QEMU.
===== runtime/pack-native.sh =====
    1: #!/usr/bin/env bash
    2: # pack-native.sh ƒ?" package iolbox as a standalone installer tarball for ANY
    3: # systemd glibc Linux server (bare metal, cloud VM, on-prem hypervisor guest
    4: # ƒ?" not the WSL2/VMware appliance rootfs the other pack-* scripts build, and
    5: # not a macOS Lima/VZ guest ƒ?" the M7 native-arm64 profile uses this same
    6: # packager with --arch arm64, then the macOS launcher's install.sh flow runs
    7: # it inside the guest). See docs/providers.md's `remote` provider and
    8: # runtime/files/native/README.txt for what ships inside.
    9: #
   10: # Unlike pack-wsl.sh/pack-vmware.sh, this does NOT consume the debootstrap
   11: # rootfs from build-rootfs.sh ƒ?" a native install runs on the OPERATOR's own
   12: # distro, whatever it already has, so this script only needs the two
   13: # binaries (supervisor, vpcs) plus the support files under
   14: # runtime/files/{,native/}. No root required to run this script.
   15: #
   16: # Output: runtime/build/iolbox-server-<version>.tar.gz (amd64, historical
   17: # name) or runtime/build/iolbox-server-<version>-linux-<arch>.tar.gz when
   18: # --arch is passed explicitly.
   19: set -euo pipefail
   20: 
   21: SCRIPT_DIR="$(cd "$(dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")" && pwd)"
   22: BUILD_DIR="${IOLBOX_BUILD_DIR:-$SCRIPT_DIR/build}"
   23: FILES_DIR="$SCRIPT_DIR/files"
   24: NATIVE_DIR="$FILES_DIR/native"
   25: 
   26: VERSION="dev"
   27: TARGET_ARCH="amd64"
   28: ARCH_EXPLICIT=0
   29: VALIDATE_ONLY=0
   30: # Same default search order build-rootfs.sh uses for the supervisor binary;
   31: # see that script's SUPERVISOR_BIN comment (matches PLAN.md repo layout +
   32: # .gitignore's /supervisor/bin/). Preferred over the rootfs copy when both
   33: # exist, per this script's brief ("prefer the explicit --supervisor-bin
   34: # flag like the other pack scripts" ƒ?" --supervisor-bin always wins).
   35: # These are recomputed after flag parsing once TARGET_ARCH is final (see
   36: # below) so --arch arm64 picks up supervisor-linux-arm64 instead of the
   37: # amd64 default.
   38: SUPERVISOR_BIN_DEFAULT_1="$SCRIPT_DIR/../supervisor/bin/supervisor-linux-$TARGET_ARCH"
   39: SUPERVISOR_BIN_DEFAULT_2="$BUILD_DIR/rootfs/opt/iolbox/supervisor"
   40: SUPERVISOR_BIN=""
   41: VPCS_BIN_DEFAULT="$BUILD_DIR/vpcs/vpcs"     # fetch-vpcs.sh's output path
   42: VPCS_BIN=""
   43: # Source, not a prebuilt binary: unlike vpcs (fetched) and supervisor (built by
   44: # build-release.sh), iolbox-toollaunch is trivial to cross-compile here with
   45: # CGO disabled, matching how runtime/build-rootfs.sh builds it (see that
   46: # script's `go build -trimpath` invocation for TOOLLAUNCH_BIN).
   47: TOOLLAUNCH_SRC_DEFAULT="$SCRIPT_DIR/../tools/iolbox-toollaunch"
   48: TOOLLAUNCH_SRC=""
   49: TOOLLAUNCH_BIN=""
   50: # Learning-tool packs (see docs/ for the P2/P3 plans). Each simple pack is a
   51: # single static Go binary that is both the pack's AF_UNIX GUI and its
   52: # lab-facing service; "pc" is the built-in netprobe pack PC/VPCS nodes require
   53: # (see supervisor/internal/server/toolpacks.go). secbench additionally ships a
   54: # directory of standalone attack binaries under tools/secbench-attacks-go.
   55: # Skippable with --no-packs for callers that only need supervisor/vpcs (e.g. a
   56: # quick IOL-only smoke build) and don't want the extra build time. Every pack
   57: # and the secbench attack binaries are cross-compiled for TARGET_ARCH, same as
   58: # supervisor/toollaunch ƒ?" a native-arm64 package must not silently ship amd64
   59: # pack binaries.
   60: SIMPLE_PACKS="aaa webserver httpclient syslog netsvc pc"
   61: SECBENCH_ATTACKS_SRC_DEFAULT="$SCRIPT_DIR/../tools/secbench-attacks-go"
   62: SKIP_PACKS=0
   63: OUT_DIR="$BUILD_DIR"
   64: 
   65: usage() {
   66:     cat <<EOF
   67: Usage: $0 [options]
   68: 
   69:   --version X.Y.Z         Version string embedded in the output filename and
   70:                            the top-level directory inside the tarball
   71:                            (default: $VERSION)
   72:   --arch ARCH              Payload architecture: amd64 (default) or arm64.
   73:                            No-argument amd64 keeps the historical package
   74:                            name/layout; an explicit --arch (including
   75:                            --arch amd64) suffixes the package name with
   76:                            -linux-<arch> and writes manifest.env.
   77:   --supervisor-bin PATH   Path to the built Go supervisor binary. Build it
   78:                            with the repo's build-release.sh (the GUI bundle
   79:                            must be embedded ƒ?" a plain 'go build' ships a
   80:                            placeholder GUI). Default: first of
   81:                              $SUPERVISOR_BIN_DEFAULT_1
   82:                              $SUPERVISOR_BIN_DEFAULT_2
   83:                            that exists.
   84:   --vpcs-bin PATH          Path to a prebuilt linux/\$TARGET_ARCH vpcs binary.
   85:                            Default: $VPCS_BIN_DEFAULT (run ./fetch-vpcs.sh
   86:                            first, or drop a binary there by hand)
   87:   --toollaunch-bin PATH    Path to a prebuilt linux/\$TARGET_ARCH
   88:                            iolbox-toollaunch binary. Default: cross-compiled
   89:                            on the fly from $TOOLLAUNCH_SRC_DEFAULT (requires
   90:                            'go' on PATH).
   91:   --no-packs               Skip building/staging the learning-tool packs
   92:                            (aaa/webserver/httpclient/syslog/netsvc/pc/secbench).
   93:                            PC/VPCS nodes will not work without them.
   94:   --build-dir DIR          Output root (default: $BUILD_DIR)
   95:   --out DIR                Where to write the tarball (default: --build-dir)
   96:   --validate-only          Validate supplied/resolved ELF architectures
   97:                            (supervisor, vpcs, toollaunch), then exit 0
   98:                            without staging or packing anything.
   99:   -h, --help               This help
  100: 
  101: Output: <out>/iolbox-server-<version>[-linux-<arch>].tar.gz
  102: EOF
  103: }
  104: 
  105: while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do
  106:     case "$1" in
  107:         --version) VERSION="$2"; shift 2 ;;
  108:         --arch) TARGET_ARCH="$2"; ARCH_EXPLICIT=1; shift 2 ;;
  109:         --arch=*) TARGET_ARCH="${1#*=}"; ARCH_EXPLICIT=1; shift ;;
  110:         --supervisor-bin) SUPERVISOR_BIN="$2"; shift 2 ;;
  111:         --vpcs-bin) VPCS_BIN="$2"; shift 2 ;;
  112:         --toollaunch-bin) TOOLLAUNCH_BIN="$2"; shift 2 ;;
  113:         --no-packs) SKIP_PACKS=1; shift ;;
  114:         --build-dir) BUILD_DIR="$2"; OUT_DIR="$BUILD_DIR"; shift 2 ;;
  115:         --out) OUT_DIR="$2"; shift 2 ;;
  116:         --validate-only) VALIDATE_ONLY=1; shift ;;
  117:         -h|--help) usage; exit 0 ;;
  118:         *) echo "unknown option: $1" >&2; usage; exit 1 ;;
  119:     esac
  120: done
  121: 
  122: case "$TARGET_ARCH" in
  123:     amd64|arm64) ;;
  124:     *) echo "pack-native: --arch must be amd64 or arm64, got '$TARGET_ARCH'" >&2; exit 1 ;;
  125: esac
  126: 
  127: # Normalize to absolute paths now that --build-dir/--out are final. Several
  128: # steps below (the tool-pack build loop in particular) build inside a `cd`'d
  129: # subshell; a relative BUILD_DIR/OUT_DIR would resolve against that subshell's
  130: # cwd instead of the caller's, silently writing packs to the wrong place and
  131: # leaving PACK_STAGE never created for the `install` calls that follow.
  132: mkdir -p "$BUILD_DIR" "$OUT_DIR"
  133: BUILD_DIR="$(cd "$BUILD_DIR" && pwd)"
  134: OUT_DIR="$(cd "$OUT_DIR" && pwd)"
  135: 
  136: # Recompute architecture-sensitive defaults after all flags have been parsed
  137: # and BUILD_DIR is absolute; this preserves the no-argument amd64 paths while
  138: # making --arch arm64 select the matching supervisor payload.
  139: SUPERVISOR_BIN_DEFAULT_1="$SCRIPT_DIR/../supervisor/bin/supervisor-linux-$TARGET_ARCH"
  140: SUPERVISOR_BIN_DEFAULT_2="$BUILD_DIR/rootfs/opt/iolbox/supervisor"
  141: VPCS_BIN_DEFAULT="$BUILD_DIR/vpcs/vpcs"
  142: 
  143: # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  144: # Resolve binary sources
  145: # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  146: if [ -z "$SUPERVISOR_BIN" ]; then
  147:     if [ -f "$SUPERVISOR_BIN_DEFAULT_1" ]; then
  148:         SUPERVISOR_BIN="$SUPERVISOR_BIN_DEFAULT_1"
  149:     elif [ -f "$SUPERVISOR_BIN_DEFAULT_2" ]; then
  150:         SUPERVISOR_BIN="$SUPERVISOR_BIN_DEFAULT_2"
  151:     fi
  152: fi
  153: 
  154: if [ -z "$SUPERVISOR_BIN" ] || [ ! -f "$SUPERVISOR_BIN" ]; then
  155:     cat >&2 <<EOF
  156: pack-native: supervisor binary not found. Checked:
  157:   $SUPERVISOR_BIN_DEFAULT_1
  158:   $SUPERVISOR_BIN_DEFAULT_2
  159: 
  160: Build it first (from the repo root):
  161:   bash build-release.sh    # -> supervisor/bin/supervisor-linux-$TARGET_ARCH
  162:                             # (embeds the GUI bundle; plain 'go build' does not)
  163: 
  164: ...or pass --supervisor-bin /path/to/binary.
  165: EOF
  166:     exit 1
  167: fi
  168: 
  169: if [ -z "$VPCS_BIN" ]; then
  170:     VPCS_BIN="$VPCS_BIN_DEFAULT"
  171: fi
  172: 
  173: # A Windows/Git-Bash cross-build workspace may not preserve the POSIX execute
  174: # bit on a transferred ELF. The archive staging below applies 0755, while
  175: # require_elf_arch immediately after this check proves the input is an ELF of
  176: # the requested architecture, so require a regular file here, not host-mode
  177: # metadata that Windows cannot represent faithfully.
  178: if [ ! -f "$VPCS_BIN" ]; then
  179:     cat >&2 <<EOF
  180: pack-native: vpcs binary not found or not a regular file at:
  181:   $VPCS_BIN
  182: 
  183: Run ./fetch-vpcs.sh first (needs network access), or drop a prebuilt
  184: linux/$TARGET_ARCH vpcs binary at that path by hand, or pass --vpcs-bin PATH.
  185: EOF
  186:     exit 1
  187: fi
  188: 
  189: if [ -z "$TOOLLAUNCH_BIN" ]; then
  190:     if [ -z "$TOOLLAUNCH_SRC" ]; then
  191:         TOOLLAUNCH_SRC="$TOOLLAUNCH_SRC_DEFAULT"
  192:     fi
  193:     if [ ! -d "$TOOLLAUNCH_SRC" ]; then
  194:         cat >&2 <<EOF
  195: pack-native: iolbox-toollaunch source not found at:
  196:   $TOOLLAUNCH_SRC
  197: 
  198: Pass --toollaunch-bin PATH to use a prebuilt linux/$TARGET_ARCH binary instead.
  199: EOF
  200:         exit 1
  201:     fi
  202:     command -v go >/dev/null 2>&1 || {
  203:         cat >&2 <<EOF
  204: pack-native: 'go' is not on PATH, needed to build iolbox-toollaunch from
  205:   $TOOLLAUNCH_SRC
  206: 
  207: Install Go, or pass --toollaunch-bin PATH to use a prebuilt linux/$TARGET_ARCH
  208: binary instead.
  209: EOF
  210:         exit 1
  211:     }
  212:     TOOLLAUNCH_BIN="$BUILD_DIR/iolbox-toollaunch-$TARGET_ARCH"
  213:     echo "== pack-native: building iolbox-toollaunch from $TOOLLAUNCH_SRC (linux/$TARGET_ARCH) =="
  214:     ( cd "$TOOLLAUNCH_SRC" && GOOS=linux GOARCH="$TARGET_ARCH" CGO_ENABLED=0 go build -trimpath -o "$TOOLLAUNCH_BIN" . )
  215: fi
  216: 
  217: if [ ! -x "$TOOLLAUNCH_BIN" ] && [ ! -f "$TOOLLAUNCH_BIN" ]; then
  218:     echo "pack-native: iolbox-toollaunch binary not found at: $TOOLLAUNCH_BIN" >&2
  219:     exit 1
  220: fi
  221: 
  222: # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  223: # Fail-closed ELF architecture validation. A native package must never be
  224: # assembled with a binary that cannot execute on the requested TARGET_ARCH ƒ?"
  225: # this replaces the earlier amd64-only, warning-only 'file' probe with a hard
  226: # gate that understands both amd64 and arm64 ELF headers, using 'file' when
  227: # present and falling back to 'readelf -h'.
  228: # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  229: require_elf_arch() {
  230:     local path="$1"
  231:     local expected="$2"
  232:     local label="$3"
  233:     local description
  234: 
  235:     if command -v file >/dev/null 2>&1; then
  236:         if ! description="$(file -b -- "$path" 2>&1)"; then
  237:             echo "$label: file failed while inspecting $path: $description" >&2
  238:             return 1
  239:         fi
  240:         case "$expected:$description" in
  241:             amd64:*"ELF 64-bit"*"x86-64"*) ;;
  242:             arm64:*"ELF 64-bit"*"ARM aarch64"*) ;;
  243:             arm64:*"ELF 64-bit"*"AArch64"*) ;;
  244:             *)
  245:                 echo "$label: $path is not a linux/$expected ELF: $description" >&2
  246:                 return 1
  247:                 ;;
  248:         esac
  249:         return 0
  250:     fi
  251: 
  252:     if command -v readelf >/dev/null 2>&1; then
  253:         if ! description="$(readelf -h "$path" 2>&1)"; then
  254:             echo "$label: readelf failed while inspecting $path: $description" >&2
  255:             return 1
  256:         fi
  257:         case "$expected:$description" in
  258:             amd64:*"Class:"*"ELF64"*"Machine:"*"X86-64"*) ;;
  259:             arm64:*"Class:"*"ELF64"*"Machine:"*"AArch64"*) ;;
  260:             *)
  261:                 echo "$label: $path is not a linux/$expected ELF (readelf header did not match)" >&2
  262:                 return 1
  263:                 ;;
  264:         esac
  265:         return 0
  266:     fi
  267: 
  268:     echo "$label: neither 'file' nor 'readelf' is available to verify $path is linux/$expected; refusing to assemble an unverified native package" >&2
  269:     return 1
  270: }
  271: 
  272: require_elf_arch "$SUPERVISOR_BIN" "$TARGET_ARCH" "pack-native"
  273: require_elf_arch "$VPCS_BIN" "$TARGET_ARCH" "pack-native"
  274: require_elf_arch "$TOOLLAUNCH_BIN" "$TARGET_ARCH" "pack-native"
  275: 
  276: if [ "$VALIDATE_ONLY" -eq 1 ]; then
  277:     echo "pack-native: architecture validation passed for linux/$TARGET_ARCH"
  278:     exit 0
  279: fi
  280: 
  281: # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  282: # Learning-tool packs (skippable with --no-packs)
  283: # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  284: PACK_STAGE="$BUILD_DIR/native-packs-$TARGET_ARCH"
  285: if [ "$SKIP_PACKS" -eq 1 ]; then
  286:     echo "== pack-native: --no-packs given, skipping tool packs (PC/VPCS nodes will not work) =="
  287: else
  288:     command -v go >/dev/null 2>&1 || {
  289:         echo "pack-native: 'go' is not on PATH, needed to build the tool packs. Pass --no-packs to skip them (PC/VPCS nodes will not work), or install Go." >&2
  290:         exit 1
  291:     }
  292:     rm -rf "$PACK_STAGE"
  293:     for pack in $SIMPLE_PACKS; do
  294:         pack_dir="$FILES_DIR/tools/packs/$pack"
  295:         [ -f "$pack_dir/pack.json" ] || { echo "pack-native: pack manifest missing: $pack_dir/pack.json" >&2; exit 1; }
  296:         echo "== pack-native: building $pack pack GUI (linux/$TARGET_ARCH) =="
  297:         ( cd "$pack_dir/gui" && GOOS=linux GOARCH="$TARGET_ARCH" CGO_ENABLED=0 go build -trimpath -o "$PACK_STAGE/$pack/$pack-gui" . )
  298:         install -m 0644 "$pack_dir/pack.json" "$PACK_STAGE/$pack/pack.json"
  299:         chmod 0755 "$PACK_STAGE/$pack/$pack-gui"
  300:     done
  301: 
  302:     echo "== pack-native: building secbench pack GUI (linux/$TARGET_ARCH) =="
  303:     secbench_dir="$FILES_DIR/tools/packs/secbench"
  304:     [ -f "$secbench_dir/pack.json" ] || { echo "pack-native: pack manifest missing: $secbench_dir/pack.json" >&2; exit 1; }
  305:     install -d -m 0755 "$PACK_STAGE/secbench/bin"
  306:     ( cd "$secbench_dir/gui" && GOOS=linux GOARCH="$TARGET_ARCH" CGO_ENABLED=0 go build -trimpath -o "$PACK_STAGE/secbench/secbench-gui" . )
  307:     install -m 0644 "$secbench_dir/pack.json" "$PACK_STAGE/secbench/pack.json"
  308: 
  309:     SECBENCH_ATTACKS_SRC="${SECBENCH_ATTACKS_SRC:-$SECBENCH_ATTACKS_SRC_DEFAULT}"
  310:     if [ ! -d "$SECBENCH_ATTACKS_SRC" ]; then
  311:         echo "pack-native: secbench attack binaries source not found: $SECBENCH_ATTACKS_SRC" >&2
  312:         exit 1
  313:     fi
  314:     echo "== pack-native: building secbench attack binaries (linux/$TARGET_ARCH) =="
  315:     ( cd "$SECBENCH_ATTACKS_SRC" && for d in cmd/*/; do
  316:         key="$(basename "$d")"
  317:         GOOS=linux GOARCH="$TARGET_ARCH" CGO_ENABLED=0 go build -trimpath -o "$PACK_STAGE/secbench/bin/$key" "./$d"
  318:     done )
  319: fi
  320: 
  321: for f in \
  322:     "$FILES_DIR/iolbox-supervisor.service" \
  323:     "$FILES_DIR/iolbox-firstboot-iourc.service" \
  324:     "$FILES_DIR/firstboot-iourc.sh" \
  325:     "$FILES_DIR/prestart-clean.sh" \
  326:     "$FILES_DIR/99-iolbox.conf" \
  327:     "$NATIVE_DIR/install.sh" \
  328:     "$NATIVE_DIR/uninstall.sh" \
  329:     "$NATIVE_DIR/README.txt" \
  330:     "$NATIVE_DIR/10-bind.conf.tmpl" \
  331:     "$NATIVE_DIR/iolbox-bind.env.local" \
  332:     "$NATIVE_DIR/iolbox-bind.env.all" \
  333:     ; do
  334:     if [ ! -f "$f" ]; then
  335:         echo "pack-native: required source file missing: $f" >&2
  336:         exit 1
  337:     fi
  338: done
  339: 
  340: # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  341: # Assemble the staging tree
  342: # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  343: if [ "$ARCH_EXPLICIT" -eq 1 ]; then
  344:     PKG_NAME="iolbox-server-$VERSION-linux-$TARGET_ARCH"
  345: else
  346:     # Preserve the historical no-argument amd64 archive/top-level name.
  347:     PKG_NAME="iolbox-server-$VERSION"
  348: fi
  349: STAGE_ROOT="$BUILD_DIR/native-stage"
  350: STAGE_DIR="$STAGE_ROOT/$PKG_NAME"
  351: 
  352: echo "== pack-native: staging $STAGE_DIR =="
  353: rm -rf "$STAGE_DIR"
  354: install -d -m 0755 "$STAGE_DIR/bin"
  355: install -d -m 0755 "$STAGE_DIR/opt-iolbox"
  356: install -d -m 0755 "$STAGE_DIR/systemd"
  357: install -d -m 0755 "$STAGE_DIR/etc"
  358: 
  359: # Binaries ƒ?" 0755 (a 0644 supervisor binary fails fork/exec; see
  360: # runtime/build-rootfs.sh's install invocation and install.sh's comment).
  361: install -m 0755 "$SUPERVISOR_BIN" "$STAGE_DIR/bin/supervisor"
  362: install -m 0755 "$VPCS_BIN" "$STAGE_DIR/bin/vpcs"
  363: install -m 0755 "$TOOLLAUNCH_BIN" "$STAGE_DIR/bin/iolbox-toollaunch"
  364: 
  365: # Learning-tool packs, staged verbatim under tools/packs/<id>/ so install.sh
  366: # can copy the whole tree into $PREFIX/tools/packs without per-pack knowledge.
  367: if [ "$SKIP_PACKS" -ne 1 ]; then
  368:     install -d -m 0755 "$STAGE_DIR/tools"
  369:     cp -R "$PACK_STAGE" "$STAGE_DIR/tools/packs"
  370: fi
  371: 
  372: # Reused verbatim from runtime/files/ ƒ?" same scripts the WSL/VMware rootfs
  373: # ships, no VMware-specific path assumptions in either (both are plain
  374: # POSIX sh operating only under /opt/iolbox, which install.sh also uses as
  375: # its default --prefix).
  376: install -m 0755 "$FILES_DIR/firstboot-iourc.sh" "$STAGE_DIR/opt-iolbox/firstboot-iourc.sh"
  377: install -m 0755 "$FILES_DIR/prestart-clean.sh" "$STAGE_DIR/opt-iolbox/prestart-clean.sh"
  378: 
  379: # Stock systemd units, byte-identical to the WSL/VMware copies ƒ?" the bind
  380: # addresses are overridden by the 10-bind.conf drop-in, not by editing the
  381: # unit itself, so this one flag set stays audited-once across every
  382: # packaging target (see runtime/README.md).
  383: install -m 0644 "$FILES_DIR/iolbox-supervisor.service" "$STAGE_DIR/systemd/iolbox-supervisor.service"
  384: install -m 0644 "$FILES_DIR/iolbox-firstboot-iourc.service" "$STAGE_DIR/systemd/iolbox-firstboot-iourc.service"
  385: install -m 0644 "$NATIVE_DIR/10-bind.conf.tmpl" "$STAGE_DIR/systemd/10-bind.conf"
  386: install -m 0644 "$NATIVE_DIR/iolbox-bind.env.local" "$STAGE_DIR/systemd/bind.env.local"
  387: install -m 0644 "$NATIVE_DIR/iolbox-bind.env.all" "$STAGE_DIR/systemd/bind.env.all"
  388: 
  389: install -m 0644 "$FILES_DIR/99-iolbox.conf" "$STAGE_DIR/etc/99-iolbox.conf"
  390: 
  391: install -m 0755 "$NATIVE_DIR/install.sh" "$STAGE_DIR/install.sh"
  392: install -m 0755 "$NATIVE_DIR/uninstall.sh" "$STAGE_DIR/uninstall.sh"
  393: # Keep the historical README content, but make the selected architecture
  394: # explicit and correct the architecture-specific prose for arm64. The
  395: # no-argument amd64 form remains the historical package name and payload
  396: # semantics; explicit architectures get distinct names and metadata.
  397: {
  398:     if [ "$TARGET_ARCH" = "arm64" ]; then
  399:         printf 'Architecture: linux/arm64 (AArch64)\n\n'
  400:         sed -e 's/linux\/amd64/linux\/arm64/g' -e 's/x86-64/AArch64/g' "$NATIVE_DIR/README.txt"
  401:     else
  402:         printf 'Architecture: linux/amd64 (x86-64)\n\n'
  403:         cat "$NATIVE_DIR/README.txt"
  404:     fi
  405: } > "$STAGE_DIR/README.txt"
  406: chmod 0644 "$STAGE_DIR/README.txt"
  407: 
  408: # manifest.env ƒ?" install.sh's fail-closed arch check reads this when present
  409: # (see runtime/files/native/install.sh). Written for every explicit --arch
  410: # invocation (including --arch amd64) so a caller that asked for a specific
  411: # architecture always gets a package that can prove what it contains; the
  412: # bare no-argument historical form stays manifest-free for byte-for-byte
  413: # compatibility with pre-M7 amd64 packages.
  414: if [ "$ARCH_EXPLICIT" -eq 1 ]; then
  415:     {
  416:         printf 'version=%s\n' "$VERSION"
  417:         printf 'os=linux\n'
  418:         printf 'arch=%s\n' "$TARGET_ARCH"
  419:         printf 'supervisor_sha256=%s\n' "$(sha256sum "$SUPERVISOR_BIN" | awk '{print $1}')"
  420:         printf 'vpcs_sha256=%s\n' "$(sha256sum "$VPCS_BIN" | awk '{print $1}')"
  421:     } > "$STAGE_DIR/manifest.env"
  422:     chmod 0644 "$STAGE_DIR/manifest.env"
  423: fi
  424: 
  425: # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  426: # Tar it up
  427: # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  428: install -d -m 0755 "$OUT_DIR"
  429: OUT_TAR="$OUT_DIR/$PKG_NAME.tar.gz"
  430: 
  431: echo "== pack-native: creating $OUT_TAR =="
  432: # GNU tar (the historical Linux-builder assumption) and bsdtar (macOS's
  433: # bundled /usr/bin/tar, which this profile's own workflow now runs this
  434: # script under directly per docs/m7-phase4-file-mapping.md) accept
  435: # different owner-zeroing flag spellings, and bsdtar has no --sort at all.
  436: # Found running this exact command on a real Mac (macOS 26.6.2/25G83,
  437: # bsdtar 3.5.3/libarchive 3.7.4): "tar: Option --sort=name is not
  438: # supported", exit 1 ƒ?" pack-native.sh had only ever been exercised on a
  439: # GNU-tar Linux builder before. Detect which flavor is on PATH and use its
  440: # real flags; --sort=name is reproducibility-nice-to-have, not correctness-
  441: # critical, so bsdtar's build simply omits it rather than gaining a second
  442: # dependency.
  443: if tar --version 2>/dev/null | grep -q GNU; then
  444:     tar \
  445:         --create --gzip \
  446:         --file "$OUT_TAR" \
  447:         --directory "$STAGE_ROOT" \
  448:         --numeric-owner \
  449:         --owner=0 --group=0 \
  450:         --sort=name \
  451:         "$PKG_NAME"
  452: else
  453:     tar \
  454:         --create --gzip \
  455:         --file "$OUT_TAR" \
  456:         --directory "$STAGE_ROOT" \
  457:         --uid 0 --gid 0 \
  458:         "$PKG_NAME"
  459: fi
  460: 
  461: rm -rf "$STAGE_ROOT"
  462: 
  463: echo "== pack-native: done =="
  464: ls -lh "$OUT_TAR"
  465: cat <<EOF
  466: 
  467: Install on the target server:
  468:   tar xzf $(basename "$OUT_TAR")
  469:   cd $PKG_NAME
  470:   sudo ./install.sh              # or: sudo ./install.sh --bind all
  471: 
  472: See runtime/files/native/README.txt (also shipped inside the tarball) for
  473: the full flag/security rundown.
  474: EOF
===== runtime/fetch-vpcs.sh =====
    1: #!/usr/bin/env bash
    2: # fetch-vpcs.sh ƒ?" clone and build the community VPCS binary with UDP-tunnel
    3: # support (the same wire format IOL's iol_wrapper uses for inter-node
    4: # links, per docs/protocol.md "Interface addressing (IOL)" ƒ?" VPCS speaks
    5: # UDP tunnels natively, no relay/hub translation needed on that side).
    6: #
    7: # Called by build-rootfs.sh, but also runnable standalone for iterating on
    8: # just the VPCS build without redoing the whole debootstrap. Network
    9: # access is required (git clone + apt build-deps); if the builder is
   10: # airgapped, see the "manual drop path" note below instead.
   11: #
   12: # Output: ./build/vpcs/vpcs  (a single static-ish ELF binary for VPCS_ARCH)
   13: set -euo pipefail
   14: 
   15: SCRIPT_DIR="$(cd "$(dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")" && pwd)"
   16: BUILD_DIR="${IOLBOX_BUILD_DIR:-$SCRIPT_DIR/build}"
   17: VPCS_SRC_DIR="$BUILD_DIR/vpcs-src"
   18: VPCS_OUT_DIR="$BUILD_DIR/vpcs"
   19: 
   20: # Upstream: the maintained community fork (the original vpcs.googlecode.com
   21: # / sourceforge project is unmaintained; GNS3's fork is what every current
   22: # IOL-adjacent lab tool, including our own PNetLab work, actually builds
   23: # from). Pinned to a tag, not a moving branch, for reproducibility ƒ?" bump
   24: # deliberately, not silently on every rebuild.
   25: VPCS_REPO="${VPCS_REPO:-https://github.com/GNS3/vpcs.git}"
   26: VPCS_REF="${VPCS_REF:-v0.8.3}"   # same release the proven iolbox-rt vpcs was built from
   27: VPCS_ARCH="${VPCS_ARCH:-amd64}"
   28: VPCS_CC="${VPCS_CC:-}"
   29: VPCS_ARCH_EXPLICIT=0
   30: 
   31: while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do
   32:     case "$1" in
   33:         --arch) VPCS_ARCH="$2"; VPCS_ARCH_EXPLICIT=1; shift 2 ;;
   34:         --arch=*) VPCS_ARCH="${1#*=}"; VPCS_ARCH_EXPLICIT=1; shift ;;
   35:         --help|-h)
   36:             cat <<EOF
   37: Usage: $0 [--arch amd64|arm64]
   38: 
   39: Build pinned GNS3 VPCS $VPCS_REF from source. The default is amd64. For an
   40: arm64 build, run on an arm64 Linux builder or set VPCS_CC to an arm64 C
   41: compiler (for example aarch64-linux-gnu-gcc) when cross-compiling.
   42: EOF
   43:             exit 0
   44:             ;;
   45:         *) echo "fetch-vpcs: unknown option: $1" >&2; exit 1 ;;
   46:     esac
   47: done
   48: 
   49: case "$VPCS_ARCH" in
   50:     amd64|arm64) ;;
   51:     *) echo "fetch-vpcs: --arch must be amd64 or arm64, got '$VPCS_ARCH'" >&2; exit 1 ;;
   52: esac
   53: 
   54: # Inspect ELF headers with the first available tool. An explicitly qualified
   55: # build must never silently accept a host-architecture binary: if the selected
   56: # inspector is unavailable or fails, the qualification fails closed. The
   57: # historical no-argument amd64 path below intentionally keeps its warning-only
   58: # behavior for compatibility with existing local builds.
   59: require_elf_arch() {
   60:     local path="$1"
   61:     local expected="$2"
   62:     local label="$3"
   63:     local description
   64: 
   65:     if command -v file >/dev/null 2>&1; then
   66:         if ! description="$(file -b -- "$path" 2>&1)"; then
   67:             echo "$label: file failed while inspecting $path: $description" >&2
   68:             return 1
   69:         fi
   70:         case "$expected:$description" in
   71:             amd64:*"ELF 64-bit"*"x86-64"*) ;;
   72:             arm64:*"ELF 64-bit"*"ARM aarch64"*) ;;
   73:             arm64:*"ELF 64-bit"*"AArch64"*) ;;
   74:             *)
   75:                 echo "$label: $path is not a linux/$expected ELF: $description" >&2
   76:                 return 1
   77:                 ;;
   78:         esac
   79:         return 0
   80:     fi
   81: 
   82:     if command -v readelf >/dev/null 2>&1; then
   83:         if ! description="$(readelf -h "$path" 2>&1)"; then
   84:             echo "$label: readelf failed while inspecting $path: $description" >&2
   85:             return 1
   86:         fi
   87:         case "$expected:$description" in
   88:             amd64:*"Class:"*"ELF64"*"Machine:"*"X86-64"*) ;;
   89:             arm64:*"Class:"*"ELF64"*"Machine:"*"AArch64"*) ;;
   90:             *)
   91:                 echo "$label: $path is not a linux/$expected ELF (readelf header did not match)" >&2
   92:                 return 1
   93:                 ;;
   94:         esac
   95:         return 0
   96:     fi
   97: 
   98:     echo "$label: either 'file' or 'readelf' is required for architecture validation" >&2
   99:     return 1
  100: }
  101: 
  102: MAKE_CC_ARGS=()
  103: if [ -n "$VPCS_CC" ]; then
  104:     MAKE_CC_ARGS+=("CC=$VPCS_CC")
  105: fi
  106: 
  107: echo "== fetch-vpcs: $VPCS_REPO @ $VPCS_REF =="
  108: if [ "$VPCS_ARCH" != amd64 ]; then
  109:     echo "fetch-vpcs: target architecture: $VPCS_ARCH"
  110: fi
  111: 
  112: # --- Manual drop path (airgapped builder) ----------------------------------
  113: # If this builder has no network access, skip this script entirely and
  114: # instead place a prebuilt linux/$VPCS_ARCH vpcs binary at:
  115: #
  116: #     runtime/build/vpcs/vpcs
  117: #
  118: # before running build-rootfs.sh (which looks for exactly that path ƒ?" see
  119: # its --vpcs-bin flag / VPCS_BIN default below). build-rootfs.sh does not
  120: # require this script to have run; it just requires the binary to exist.
  121: # -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  122: 
  123: if [ -x "$VPCS_OUT_DIR/vpcs" ]; then
  124:     if [ "$VPCS_ARCH_EXPLICIT" -eq 1 ]; then
  125:         require_elf_arch "$VPCS_OUT_DIR/vpcs" "$VPCS_ARCH" "fetch-vpcs"
  126:     fi
  127:     echo "fetch-vpcs: $VPCS_OUT_DIR/vpcs already built, skipping (delete it to force a rebuild)"
  128:     exit 0
  129: fi
  130: 
  131: command -v git >/dev/null 2>&1 || { echo "fetch-vpcs: git is required" >&2; exit 1; }
  132: command -v make >/dev/null 2>&1 || { echo "fetch-vpcs: make is required (apt install build-essential)" >&2; exit 1; }
  133: command -v cc >/dev/null 2>&1 || command -v gcc >/dev/null 2>&1 || {
  134:     echo "fetch-vpcs: a C compiler is required (apt install build-essential)" >&2
  135:     exit 1
  136: }
  137: 
  138: mkdir -p "$BUILD_DIR"
  139: 
  140: if [ -d "$VPCS_SRC_DIR/.git" ]; then
  141:     echo "fetch-vpcs: reusing existing clone at $VPCS_SRC_DIR"
  142:     git -C "$VPCS_SRC_DIR" fetch --tags origin
  143: else
  144:     rm -rf "$VPCS_SRC_DIR"
  145:     git clone --branch "$VPCS_REF" --depth 1 "$VPCS_REPO" "$VPCS_SRC_DIR" \
  146:         || {
  147:             # Tag checkout can fail with --depth 1 --branch if the ref is a
  148:             # commit rather than a tag/branch; fall back to a full clone.
  149:             echo "fetch-vpcs: shallow clone of ref '$VPCS_REF' failed, retrying with full clone"
  150:             rm -rf "$VPCS_SRC_DIR"
  151:             git clone "$VPCS_REPO" "$VPCS_SRC_DIR"
  152:         }
  153: fi
  154: 
  155: git -C "$VPCS_SRC_DIR" checkout "$VPCS_REF"
  156: 
  157: # The GNS3/vpcs source layout builds from src/ with per-OS makefiles ƒ?"
  158: # src/Makefile.linux is the one that matters here (PROVEN: the reference
  159: # iolbox-rt VM's vpcs was built exactly this way). Some tags also carry a
  160: # plain src/Makefile or src/unix/Makefile.linux; try the proven layout
  161: # first, then the historical ones.
  162: pushd "$VPCS_SRC_DIR/src" >/dev/null
  163: 
  164: echo "fetch-vpcs: building..."
  165: # CFLAGS/LDFLAGS note: the binary lands inside a Debian-bookworm rootfs
  166: # whose glibc may be OLDER than this builder's (e.g. building on Ubuntu
  167: # 24.04/glibc 2.39 for bookworm/glibc 2.36). A dynamic binary built here
  168: # can pick up newer versioned symbols and fail to start in the rootfs, so
  169: # link vpcs statically ƒ?" it's a small, old-school C program and takes a
  170: # static glibc link without trouble.
  171: if [ -f Makefile.linux ]; then
  172:     make -f Makefile.linux "${MAKE_CC_ARGS[@]}" LDFLAGS="-static -lpthread -lutil"
  173: elif [ -f Makefile ]; then
  174:     make "${MAKE_CC_ARGS[@]}" LDFLAGS="-static -lpthread -lutil"
  175: elif [ -f unix/Makefile.linux ]; then
  176:     make -C unix -f Makefile.linux "${MAKE_CC_ARGS[@]}" LDFLAGS="-static -lpthread -lutil"
  177: else
  178:     echo "fetch-vpcs: no recognizable Makefile under $VPCS_SRC_DIR/src" >&2
  179:     exit 1
  180: fi
  181: 
  182: popd >/dev/null
  183: 
  184: BUILT_BIN=$(find "$VPCS_SRC_DIR" -maxdepth 3 -type f -name vpcs -perm -u+x | head -n1)
  185: if [ -z "$BUILT_BIN" ]; then
  186:     echo "fetch-vpcs: build finished but no 'vpcs' executable was found under $VPCS_SRC_DIR" >&2
  187:     exit 1
  188: fi
  189: 
  190: mkdir -p "$VPCS_OUT_DIR"
  191: cp "$BUILT_BIN" "$VPCS_OUT_DIR/vpcs"
  192: chmod 0755 "$VPCS_OUT_DIR/vpcs"
  193: 
  194: # Sanity check: must be a Linux ELF for the requested architecture, since it's
  195: # going straight into a matching rootfs. This catches a native build made for
  196: # the builder's architecture when a cross compiler was intended.
  197: if command -v file >/dev/null 2>&1; then
  198:     FILE_OUT="$(file -b "$VPCS_OUT_DIR/vpcs")"
  199:     echo "fetch-vpcs: built $VPCS_OUT_DIR/vpcs ($FILE_OUT)"
  200:     if [ "$VPCS_ARCH_EXPLICIT" -eq 1 ]; then
  201:         require_elf_arch "$VPCS_OUT_DIR/vpcs" "$VPCS_ARCH" "fetch-vpcs"
  202:     elif [ "$VPCS_ARCH" = amd64 ]; then
  203:         case "$FILE_OUT" in
  204:             *"ELF 64-bit"*"x86-64"*) : ;;
  205:             *) echo "fetch-vpcs: WARNING - binary does not look like linux/amd64 ELF64: $FILE_OUT" >&2 ;;
  206:         esac
  207:     else
  208:         case "$FILE_OUT" in
  209:             *"ELF 64-bit"*"ARM aarch64"*) : ;;
  210:             *) echo "fetch-vpcs: WARNING - binary does not look like linux/arm64 ELF64: $FILE_OUT" >&2 ;;
  211:         esac
  212:     fi
  213:     case "$FILE_OUT" in
  214:         *"statically linked"*) : ;;
  215:         *) echo "fetch-vpcs: WARNING - binary is NOT statically linked; it may fail inside the rootfs if the builder's glibc is newer than bookworm's" >&2 ;;
  216:     esac
  217: else
  218:     echo "fetch-vpcs: built $VPCS_OUT_DIR/vpcs ('file' not available to verify arch)"
  219: fi
  220: 
  221: echo "== fetch-vpcs: done -> $VPCS_OUT_DIR/vpcs =="
===== build-release.sh =====
    1: #!/usr/bin/env bash
    2: # Build a deployable supervisor binary WITH the real GUI embedded.
    3: #
    4: # The embed dir (supervisor/internal/web/dist) holds only a committed
    5: # placeholder in git; the real Svelte bundle is generated and gitignored. So a
    6: # plain `go build` ships the placeholder ("GUI not bundled in this build").
    7: # ALWAYS build the binary through this script (or run build:embed first) ƒ?" it
    8: # is the single source of truth for a deployable binary.
    9: set -euo pipefail
   10: cd "$(dirname "$0")"
   11: 
   12: # Capture the git describe BEFORE any subshell cd's around, so the version
   13: # always reflects the repo commit being built, not wherever go build runs from.
   14: VERSION="$(git describe --tags --always --dirty)"
   15: echo "==> stamping version: $VERSION"
   16: 
   17: echo "==> building GUI bundle into supervisor/internal/web/dist"
   18: ( cd app && npm run build:embed >/dev/null )
   19: 
   20: echo "==> cross-compiling linux/amd64 supervisor (GUI embedded)"
   21: ( cd supervisor && GOOS=linux GOARCH=amd64 go build -ldflags "-X main.version=$VERSION" -o bin/supervisor-linux-amd64 ./cmd/supervisor )
   22: 
   23: # Sanity: the deployed index.html must NOT be the placeholder.
   24: if grep -q "not bundled" supervisor/internal/web/dist/index.html; then
   25:   echo "ERROR: embed dir still holds the placeholder ƒ?" build:embed did not run" >&2
   26:   exit 1
   27: fi
   28: 
   29: echo "==> restoring the committed placeholder so the repo stays clean"
   30: git checkout -- supervisor/internal/web/dist/index.html 2>/dev/null || true
   31: 
   32: ls -la supervisor/bin/supervisor-linux-amd64
   33: echo "OK: supervisor/bin/supervisor-linux-amd64 has the real GUI embedded"
===== THIRD_PARTY.md =====
    1: # Third-party software distributed with iolbox
    2: 
    3: iolbox bundles the following third-party components. Their licenses are their
    4: own; this file records what we ship, from where, pinned to an exact version and
    5: checksum, and how we comply with each license.
    6: 
    7: ---
    8: 
    9: ## Apple Silicon macOS archive
   10: 
   11: The `iolbox-macos-arm64.tar.gz` archive contains the Go `iolbox` launcher, the
   12: exact native Linux payload produced by `runtime/pack-native.sh`, the locked
   13: Lima profile/provisioner files, and this notice. It contains no guest disk,
   14: hypervisor, Lima installation, or Cisco software.
   15: 
   16: ### Separately installed and fetched components
   17: 
   18: - Lima is an Apache-2.0 licensed, user-installed prerequisite. iolbox neither
   19:   redistributes nor installs, upgrades, or removes Lima.
   20: - Lima downloads the digest-locked Debian 13/trixie default or Ubuntu
   21:   22.04/Jammy compatibility guest image. The guest image, apt package indexes,
   22:   and guest packages are fetched at provisioning time and are not embedded in
   23:   the archive.
   24: - Users supply any legally held IOL/IOU image. iolbox does not distribute or
   25:   depend on Cisco software; x86_64 IOL is the only supported IOL architecture
   26:   in this Rosetta profile. i386/i86bi and arm64-native IOL are unsupported.
   27: 
   28: ### VPCS binary redistributed inside the native payload
   29: 
   30: `runtime/fetch-vpcs.sh` checks out the GNS3 VPCS project at the immutable
   31: `v0.8.3` ref (release commit `3870ae8`) and builds its Linux/amd64 binary;
   32: `runtime/pack-native.sh` places that binary at `bin/vpcs` in the native
   33: payload. The upstream project is BSD-2-Clause licensed:
   34: 
   35: - Source repository: <https://github.com/GNS3/vpcs>
   36: - Pinned source archive: <https://github.com/GNS3/vpcs/archive/refs/tags/v0.8.3.tar.gz>
   37: - Pinned release: <https://github.com/GNS3/vpcs/releases/tag/v0.8.3>
   38: - License: BSD-2-Clause, as identified by the upstream repository
   39: 
   40: The archive redistributes the resulting binary, not a moving branch checkout.
   41: The source URL and ref above are the corresponding source offer for this
   42: version; the build recipe and compiler/link flags are recorded in
   43: `runtime/fetch-vpcs.sh`.
   44: 
   45: QEMU is **not** in the Apple Silicon archive. The QEMU notice below applies
   46: only to the separate Windows launcher bundle and must not be read as a claim
   47: that the Mac artifact contains QEMU.
   48: 
   49: ---
   50: 
   51: ## QEMU (Windows build) ƒ?" the `qemu` compatibility backend
   52: 
   53: The Windows launcher (`tools/iolab-launcher`) ships a trimmed copy of QEMU so the
   54: `qemu` backend (software-emulated TCG, see `docs/providers.md` and
   55: `runtime/qemu-compat.md`) works on any Windows machine with zero prerequisites.
   56: 
   57: ### Pinned source
   58: 
   59: | Field | Value |
   60: |---|---|
   61: | Upstream | Stefan Weil's official QEMU-for-Windows builds ƒ?" <https://qemu.weilnetz.de/w64/> |
   62: | File | `qemu-w64-setup-20260501.exe` |
   63: | Full URL | <https://qemu.weilnetz.de/w64/qemu-w64-setup-20260501.exe> |
   64: | QEMU version | 11.0.0 release build (reports `11.0.50 (v11.0.0-12631-g54e84cdc7a)`) |
   65: | Installer size | 190 MB (198,760,632 bytes) |
   66: | **SHA-256 (installer, verified by download)** | `a8b29572afb4c6ad024b7de129c81033e9fd191b9e054e3a52ea0bed24ac19ef` |
   67: 
   68: `qemu.weilnetz.de` is the canonical upstream-linked Windows build (referenced
   69: from qemu.org's download page). Note: recent installers are Authenticode-signed
   70: with an **expired certificate** ƒ?" Windows SmartScreen may warn on the installer.
   71: This does not affect the extracted binaries we redistribute (we extract, we do
   72: not run the installer on user machines).
   73: 
   74: ### How the redistributable is produced (extraction, not install)
   75: 
   76: The upstream `.exe` is an NSIS installer. We do **not** run it on user machines;
   77: we extract it once and ship a trimmed tree:
   78: 
   79: ```sh
   80: # 1. verify the pinned installer
   81: sha256sum qemu-w64-setup-20260501.exe
   82: #   -> a8b29572afb4c6ad024b7de129c81033e9fd191b9e054e3a52ea0bed24ac19ef
   83: 
   84: # 2. extract with 7-Zip (NSIS archive)
   85: "C:\Program Files\7-Zip\7z.exe" x -oqemu-extract qemu-w64-setup-20260501.exe
   86: 
   87: # 3. trim to the headless x86_64 target:
   88: #    - keep  qemu-system-x86_64.exe  + ALL *.dll (the runtime DLL set)
   89: #    - keep  share/keymaps/  and the x86/i386 firmware blobs
   90: #            (bios*.bin, vgabios*.bin, kvmvapic.bin, linuxboot*/multiboot*/pvh,
   91: #             *-virtio.rom + pxe-*.rom, edk2-i386/x86_64 *.fd)
   92: #    - drop  the other 55 qemu-system-*.exe targets, share/doc, and the
   93: #            non-x86 firmware (openbios-*, edk2-aarch64/arm, dtb, ...)
   94: ```
   95: 
   96: Result: a `qemu/` directory of ~175 MB. Verified standalone on a clean box:
   97: `qemu-system-x86_64.exe -version` and `-accel help` (lists `tcg` + `whpx`) both
   98: run, so the DLL set is complete.
   99: 
  100: ### Layout shipped next to the launcher exe
  101: 
  102: ```
  103: iolbox-launcher.exe
  104: iolbox-disk.qcow2            (the runtime disk ƒ?" a separate release asset, see below)
  105: qemu/
  106:   qemu-system-x86_64.exe
  107:   *.dll                     (the runtime DLLs)
  108:   share/
  109:     keymaps/
  110:     bios*.bin, vgabios*.bin, kvmvapic.bin, linuxboot*.bin, ...
  111: ```
  112: 
  113: The launcher looks for `qemu/qemu-system-x86_64.exe` and `iolbox-disk.qcow2`
  114: relative to its own exe (`--qemu` / `--disk` override for dev). QEMU resolves its
  115: BIOS/keymap data from the `share/` dir next to its exe automatically.
  116: 
  117: The `qemu/` directory and `*.qcow2` are **git-ignored** (see
  118: `tools/iolab-launcher/.gitignore`) ƒ?" they are release-time assets staged into the
  119: bundle, never committed to the repo.
  120: 
  121: ### License / GPL compliance
  122: 
  123: QEMU is licensed under the **GNU GPL v2** (with some components under LGPL v2.1
  124: and other compatible licenses). We redistribute unmodified upstream binaries.
  125: 
  126: - The upstream binaries carry their license texts (`COPYING`, `COPYING.LIB`) in
  127:   the installer; include a copy of `COPYING`/`COPYING.LIB` in the shipped `qemu/`
  128:   directory (or link to them from the release notes) when assembling a release.
  129: - **Written offer for source:** the corresponding source for this exact build is
  130:   published by the upstream packager at the QEMU source page,
  131:   <https://www.qemu.org/download/#source> (release 11.0.0), mirrored from
  132:   <https://qemu.weilnetz.de/>. iolbox distributes QEMU **unmodified**; the source
  133:   offer therefore points at upstream. Include this URL in the release notes /
  134:   About page alongside the version above so the offer travels with the binary.
  135: 
  136: ### The iolbox runtime disk (`iolbox-disk.qcow2`)
  137: 
  138: Not third-party ƒ?" it is built from `runtime/` (Debian-slim rootfs + the iolbox
  139: supervisor) by `runtime/pack-qemu.sh`. It contains **no Cisco software**; IOL
  140: images are supplied by the user at runtime via the GUI. Debian's own components
  141: inside the rootfs are under their respective licenses (Debian is DFSG-free);
  142: this is the same rootfs the WSL2 and VMware artifacts ship, already covered by
  143: the runtime build.
===== tools/iolab-launcher/macos_lima.go =====
    1: package main
    2: 
    3: import (
    4: 	"bytes"
    5: 	"context"
    6: 	"errors"
    7: 	"fmt"
    8: 	"io"
    9: 	"os"
   10: 	"os/exec"
   11: 	"path/filepath"
   12: 	"regexp"
   13: 	"runtime"
   14: 	"sort"
   15: 	"strconv"
   16: 	"strings"
   17: 	"time"
   18: )
   19: 
   20: type commandRunner interface {
   21: 	Run(context.Context, string, ...string) ([]byte, error)
   22: }
   23: 
   24: type execRunner struct{}
   25: 
   26: func (execRunner) Run(ctx context.Context, path string, args ...string) ([]byte, error) {
   27: 	cmd := exec.CommandContext(ctx, path, args...)
   28: 	return cmd.CombinedOutput()
   29: }
   30: 
   31: type limaInfo struct {
   32: 	Path       string
   33: 	RawVersion string
   34: 	Version    string
   35: 	VersionErr error
   36: }
   37: 
   38: type limaClient struct {
   39: 	info           limaInfo
   40: 	runner         commandRunner
   41: 	instanceConfig func(string) ([]byte, error)
   42: }
   43: 
   44: type machineInfo struct {
   45: 	Name  string
   46: 	State string
   47: }
   48: 
   49: func (l *limaClient) run(ctx context.Context, args ...string) ([]byte, error) {
   50: 	return l.runner.Run(ctx, l.info.Path, args...)
   51: }
   52: 
   53: func isExecutable(path string) bool {
   54: 	st, err := os.Stat(path)
   55: 	if err != nil || st.IsDir() {
   56: 		return false
   57: 	}
   58: 	if runtime.GOOS == "windows" {
   59: 		return true
   60: 	}
   61: 	return st.Mode()&0o111 != 0
   62: }
   63: 
   64: func discoverLimactl(override, env string, lookPath func(string) (string, error)) (string, error) {
   65: 	if override != "" {
   66: 		if isExecutable(override) {
   67: 			return override, nil
   68: 		}
   69: 		return "", fmt.Errorf("configured --limactl is not executable: %s", override)
   70: 	}
   71: 	if env != "" {
   72: 		if isExecutable(env) {
   73: 			return env, nil
   74: 		}
   75: 		return "", fmt.Errorf("configured LIMACTL is not executable: %s", env)
   76: 	}
   77: 	for _, candidate := range []string{"/opt/homebrew/bin/limactl", "/usr/local/bin/limactl"} {
   78: 		if isExecutable(candidate) {
   79: 			return candidate, nil
   80: 		}
   81: 	}
   82: 	if lookPath == nil {
   83: 		lookPath = exec.LookPath
   84: 	}
   85: 	path, err := lookPath("limactl")
   86: 	if err == nil && path != "" {
   87: 		return path, nil
   88: 	}
   89: 	return "", fmt.Errorf("Lima was not found; set --limactl or LIMACTL")
   90: }
   91: 
   92: var limaVersionPattern = regexp.MustCompile(`\b([0-9]+\.[0-9]+\.[0-9]+)\b`)
   93: 
   94: func parseLimaVersion(raw string) string {
   95: 	match := limaVersionPattern.FindStringSubmatch(raw)
   96: 	if match == nil {
   97: 		return ""
   98: 	}
   99: 	return match[1]
  100: }
  101: 
  102: func collectLimaInfo(ctx context.Context, path string, runner commandRunner) limaInfo {
  103: 	if runner == nil {
  104: 		runner = execRunner{}
  105: 	}
  106: 	info := limaInfo{Path: path}
  107: 	output, err := runner.Run(ctx, path, "--version")
  108: 	info.RawVersion = string(output)
  109: 	info.Version = parseLimaVersion(info.RawVersion)
  110: 	if err != nil {
  111: 		info.VersionErr = err
  112: 	}
  113: 	return info
  114: }
  115: 
  116: // isLimaLogNoise recognizes limactl's own logrus-style diagnostic lines
  117: // (e.g. `time="2026-08-19T14:23:44-04:00" level=warning msg="No instance
  118: // found. Run \`limactl create\` to create an instance."`), which land in
  119: // this parser's input because execRunner.Run uses CombinedOutput (stdout+
  120: // stderr merged) and limactl writes that particular warning to stderr.
  121: // Found on real hardware (2026-08-19, physical Mac): every M1-M6 session up
  122: // to now ran `limactl list` against ~/.lima, which by then already had a
  123: // machine registered from earlier development, so this line never
  124: // appeared. The very first Phase 4 run against a genuinely empty, isolated
  125: // LIMA_HOME (exactly the isolation scenario plan section 10 item 4 asks
  126: // for) hit it immediately: "iolbox-launcher: invalid Lima machine listing
  127: // line 1: ...No instance found...". A truly malformed data line (wrong
  128: // field count, empty name/state) must still fail closed; only limactl's own
  129: // recognizable log-line shape is treated as noise.
  130: func isLimaLogNoise(line string) bool {
  131: 	return strings.HasPrefix(line, `time="`) && strings.Contains(line, "level=")
  132: }
  133: 
  134: func parseMachineListing(output string) ([]machineInfo, error) {
  135: 	var machines []machineInfo
  136: 	for lineNo, line := range strings.Split(strings.ReplaceAll(output, "\r\n", "\n"), "\n") {
  137: 		line = strings.TrimSpace(line)
  138: 		if line == "" || isLimaLogNoise(line) {
  139: 			continue
  140: 		}
  141: 		fields := strings.Split(line, "|")
  142: 		if len(fields) != 2 || fields[0] == "" || fields[1] == "" {
  143: 			return nil, fmt.Errorf("invalid Lima machine listing line %d: %q", lineNo+1, line)
  144: 		}
  145: 		machines = append(machines, machineInfo{Name: fields[0], State: fields[1]})
  146: 	}
  147: 	return machines, nil
  148: }
