   85: packaging/macos/guest/30-canary-native.sh           -> guest/30-canary-native.sh
   86: packaging/macos/guest/40-install-payload-native.sh  -> guest/40-install-payload-native.sh
   87: packaging/macos/guest/50-verify-native.sh           -> guest/50-verify-native.sh
   88: ```
   89: 
   90: (`20-kernel-hold-debian.sh` is shared with `debian13` and is already shipped.)
   91: 
   92: **Why they must land as a set.** `nativePreflight` validates the *pin file*
   93: only (`digests` check). It does **not** stat the YAML template or the guest
   94: scripts ƒ?" those are validated later in `loadMacOSProfile`, which returns
   95: `codedError(exitUsage, "profile asset is missing: %s")`. That is a **hard
   96: exit, not a fallback**. So shipping the pin file without the YAML would turn
   97: today's graceful `auto-fallback-rosetta` into a hard exit 1 for every
   98: Apple Silicon user. All six, or none.
   99: 
  100: ### 1.4 The payload-selection hazard (the sharpest finding)
  101: 
  102: `selectPayload` sorts candidates newest-mtime-first, tie-breaking on
  103: lexicographic path:
  104: 
  105: ```go
  106: sort.Slice(candidates, func(i, j int) bool {
  107:     if candidates[i].MTime.Equal(candidates[j].MTime) {
  108:         return candidates[i].Path < candidates[j].Path
  109:     }
  110:     return candidates[i].MTime.After(candidates[j].MTime)
  111: })
  112: ```
  113: 
  114: `pack-release.sh` stamps **every** archive member with the same
  115: `--mtime="@${SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH}"`. Both GNU tar and macOS bsdtar restore
  116: mtimes on extract. So in an extracted two-payload archive the mtimes are
  117: *equal* and the lexicographic tie-break decides ƒ?" and
  118: `iolbox-server-v0.5.3-linux-arm64.tar.gz` sorts **before**
  119: `iolbox-server-v0.5.3.tar.gz`, because `-` (0x2D) < `.` (0x2E).
  120: 
  121: **Consequence if we ship two payloads without touching the launcher: every
  122: Rosetta user gets the arm64 payload staged into their amd64 guest.** This is
  123: not a theoretical race; it is deterministic and it breaks the existing,
  124: working path. Profile-aware selection is a correctness prerequisite of
  125: shipping the second payload, not an enhancement.
  126: 
  127: ### 1.5 Ordering ƒ?" why keying off the resolved profile is sound
  128: 
  129: `runDarwinCLI` order is unambiguous:
  130: 
  131: 1. `resolveAssetRoot` ƒ+' 2. `loadProfileTableOnly` ƒ+' 3. `collectHostFacts` ƒ+'
  132: 4. **`resolveProfileSelection`** (fallback decided and final here) ƒ+'
  133: 5. fallback message ƒ+' 6. `loadMacOSProfile` ƒ+' ƒ?İ ƒ+'
  134: 9. **`selectPayload`** ƒ+' 10. `runProvision`/`stageFiles`.
  135: 
  136: The payload is consumed **strictly after** the fallback decision is final,
  137: including `auto-fallback-rosetta`. Nothing about the payload feeds back into
  138: the profile decision. So passing the resolved profile into `selectPayload`
  139: introduces no ordering problem and no new failure mode.
  140: 
  141: `resolveProfileSelection`'s real semantics, which the selection logic must
  142: respect:
  143: 
  144: - **explicit** `--profile native-arm64` ƒ+' preflight failure is a **hard
  145:   error**, never downgraded ("refusing to fall back (fail-closed)").
  146: - **persisted** `native-arm64` ƒ+' preflight failure degrades to
  147:   `persisted-fallback-rosetta`.
  148: - **auto** ƒ+' prefers native (post-`e2ffe34`); any native failure degrades to
  149:   `auto-fallback-rosetta`. **Auto never errors on a native failure.**
  150: 
  151: In every one of those branches `selection.ProfileName` is the final answer by
  152: the time `selectPayload` runs. Keying on it is correct for all five sources
  153: (`explicit-flag`, `persisted`, `persisted-fallback-rosetta`, `auto-native`,
  154: `auto-fallback-rosetta`).
  155: 
  156: ---
  157: 
  158: ## 2. Decision: archive layout (additive, Rosetta byte-unchanged)
  159: 
  160: The archive gains **one** member:
  161: 
  162: ```
  163: iolbox-macos-arm64/iolbox-server-<version>.tar.gz             (amd64, UNCHANGED)
  164: iolbox-macos-arm64/iolbox-server-<version>-linux-arm64.tar.gz (arm64, NEW)
  165: ```
  166: 
  167: plus the six profile assets from A1.3.
  168: 
  169: **The amd64 payload keeps its historical untagged name and is built exactly
  170: as today ƒ?" no `--arch` flag.** This is deliberate and is the single most
  171: important constraint in this plan. Passing `--arch amd64` would flip
  172: `pack-native.sh` into `ARCH_EXPLICIT=1`, renaming the tarball to
  173: `iolbox-server-<v>-linux-amd64.tar.gz` and adding a `manifest.env` that
  174: `install.sh` then arch-checks. That would be exactly the "silently expanding
  175: scope beyond adding native-arm64 as a release artifact" failure mode. Rejected.
  176: 
  177: The arm64 name is not invented here ƒ?" it is what `pack-native.sh --arch arm64`
  178: already emits.
  179: 
  180: **Rejected alternative: a second archive** (`iolbox-macos-arm64-native.tar.gz`).
  181: It would double the download matrix and force users to pick an architecture
  182: *before* the launcher has run its preflight ƒ?" which is precisely the decision
  183: `resolveProfileSelection` exists to make for them, on their machine, with
  184: fallback. One archive, two payloads, launcher decides. Keep it.
  185: 
  186: ---
  187: 
  188: ## 3. `release.yml` ƒ?" `build-linux` changes
  189: 
  190: ### 3.1 arm64 supervisor
  191: 
  192: `build-release.sh` is hardcoded to `GOARCH=amd64`. It gains an `--arch` flag
  193: accepting a comma-separated list; default `amd64` so the no-argument
  194: behaviour is byte-identical:
  195: 
  196: ```sh
  197: bash build-release.sh --arch amd64,arm64
  198: # -> supervisor/bin/supervisor-linux-amd64
  199: #    supervisor/bin/supervisor-linux-arm64
  200: ```
  201: 
  202: **One `npm run build:embed`, N compiles.** This is the reason to put the flag
  203: in `build-release.sh` rather than adding a bare `go build` step in the
  204: workflow, and the reason not to use a separate `ubuntu-24.04-arm` runner job:
  205: 
  206: - A bare `go build ./cmd/supervisor` in the workflow would ship the committed
  207:   placeholder GUI. `build-release.sh`'s header says so in as many words, and
  208:   the script restores the placeholder via `git checkout --` when it finishes,
  209:   so a later step in the same job would silently get the placeholder. This is
  210:   a documented past incident; do not re-open it.
  211: - A separate arm64 runner job would need its own `npm ci` + `build:embed`,
  212:   and nothing guarantees the two Vite bundles are byte-identical. Building
  213:   both supervisors from one embed *guarantees* both payloads carry the same
  214:   GUI. That is a correctness argument, not a convenience one.
  215: 
  216: The placeholder assertion runs once, after the single embed, before any
  217: compile.
  218: 
  219: ### 3.2 arm64 VPCS
  220: 
  221: VPCS is C, so it needs a real cross toolchain:
  222: 
  223: ```yaml
  224: - name: Install rootfs/packaging build deps
  225:   run: sudo apt-get install -y debootstrap zstd build-essential gcc-aarch64-linux-gnu
  226: ```
  227: 
  228: ```sh
  229: IOLBOX_BUILD_DIR=runtime/build-arm64 VPCS_CC=aarch64-linux-gnu-gcc \
  230:   runtime/fetch-vpcs.sh --arch arm64
  231: ```
  232: 
  233: **The separate `IOLBOX_BUILD_DIR` is load-bearing.** `fetch-vpcs.sh` writes to
  234: `$BUILD_DIR/vpcs/vpcs` for *both* architectures and short-circuits with
  235: `if [ -x "$VPCS_OUT_DIR/vpcs" ]; then ... exit 0`. Reusing `runtime/build`
  236: would make the arm64 invocation a silent no-op that leaves the amd64 binary
  237: in place ƒ?" and `pack-native.sh --arch arm64` would then hard-fail on
  238: `require_elf_arch` (correctly, but confusingly). A distinct build dir avoids
  239: the collision with **no change to `fetch-vpcs.sh`**.
  240: 
  241: `--arch arm64` sets `VPCS_ARCH_EXPLICIT=1`, so `fetch-vpcs.sh` fails closed on
  242: a wrong ELF rather than warning. Good.
  243: 
  244: Residual risk: the static link (`LDFLAGS="-static -lpthread -lutil"`) under
  245: `aarch64-linux-gnu-gcc`. Ubuntu's `gcc-aarch64-linux-gnu` ships a static
  246: libc, so this is expected to work, but it is the one genuinely new build step
  247: in this plan and it is unverifiable on the Windows workstation (A9).
  248: 
  249: ### 3.3 arm64 payload
  250: 
  251: ```sh
  252: runtime/pack-native.sh --arch arm64 \
  253:     --supervisor-bin supervisor/bin/supervisor-linux-arm64 \
  254:     --build-dir runtime/build-arm64 \
  255:     --out runtime/build \
  256:     --version "$VERSION"
  257: ```
  258: 
  259: `--build-dir runtime/build-arm64` keeps the arm64 vpcs, the cross-built
  260: toollaunch, and the arm64 tool-pack stage separate from the amd64 ones;
  261: `--out runtime/build` puts the finished tarball beside the amd64 one so the
  262: existing checksum and upload globs pick it up.
  263: 
  264: `pack-native.sh` already cross-compiles toollaunch, all six simple packs, the
  265: secbench GUI, and every secbench attack binary for `TARGET_ARCH` with
  266: `CGO_ENABLED=0`, and already runs `require_elf_arch` on supervisor/vpcs/
  267: toollaunch. **No change to `pack-native.sh` is needed.**
  268: 
  269: ### 3.4 Checksums and upload ƒ?" no change needed
  270: 
  271: ```sh
  272: sha256sum iolbox-rootfs.tar iolbox-ct-*.tar.zst iolbox-server-*.tar.gz > SHA256SUMS-ci.txt
  273: ```
  274: 
  275: The existing `iolbox-server-*.tar.gz` glob already matches both payloads, as
  276: does the `upload-artifact` path glob. Nothing to change ƒ?" verify in review
  277: that this is genuinely true and not wishful.
  278: 
  279: ---
  280: 
  281: ## 4. `release.yml` ƒ?" `build-macos` changes
  282: 
  283: The existing "Select and verify exact build-linux payload" step is
  284: generalised into a small bash function invoked twice (amd64, arm64) rather
  285: than duplicated, because its awk-based manifest validation is the security-
  286: relevant part and must not drift between the two copies. It keeps its current
  287: guarantees for each payload:
  288: 
  289: - exactly one file matching the expected name,
  290: - exactly one `SHA256SUMS-ci.txt`,
  291: - the manifest is well-formed (every line 2 fields, 64 hex chars),
  292: - exactly one line for this basename,
  293: - `sha256sum -c` passes.
  294: 
  295: Outputs become `path`/`sha256` and `arm64-path`/`arm64-sha256`.
  296: 
  297: `pack-release.sh` is then called with both pairs (A5), and
  298: `release-layout-test.sh` with both pairs (A6).
  299: 
  300: ---
  301: 
  302: ## 5. `pack-release.sh` changes
  303: 
  304: New required flags, named by **architecture** not by "native":
  305: 
  306: ```
  307: --payload-arm64 PATH --payload-arm64-sha256 SHA256
  308: ```
  309: 
  310: Rationale for the name: "native" is already overloaded in this repo ƒ?"
  311: `pack-native.sh` builds the "native (systemd) server tarball" for *both*
  312: architectures, while `native-arm64` is a macOS *profile*. `--native-payload`
  313: would be genuinely ambiguous at the call site. `--payload-arm64` is not.
  314: 
  315: The existing `--payload`/`--payload-sha256` keep their exact current meaning
  316: (the amd64/Rosetta payload) and their exact current basename check.
  317: 
  318: **Decision: the arm64 payload is REQUIRED, not optional.** Argument: with the
  319: six profile assets shipped, `nativePreflight` passes on a qualifying host and
  320: `auto` selects native. If the arm64 payload were absent, `selectPayload` would
  321: fail at step 9 ƒ?" *after* the profile is locked in, with no fallback path ƒ?"
  322: producing a hard preflight error on a machine that was working yesterday. An
  323: optional flag makes it possible for CI to emit that archive. Making it
  324: required means the packer physically cannot build a half-native archive. The
  325: cost is that there is no "emergency Rosetta-only cut" escape hatch; if that is
  326: ever needed the honest fix is a flag that omits the six profile assets *and*
  327: the payload together, which is not built now (YAGNI, and it would need its own
  328: layout contract).
  329: 
  330: Mechanical changes:
  331: 
  332: | Constant | From | To |
  333: |---|---|---|
  334: | manifest entry count | 18 | 24 |
  335: | `required_dest` list | 18 destinations | +6 native destinations |
  336: | `expected` staged member tree | 25 lines | +7 lines (6 assets + arm64 payload) |
  337: | staged file count before `SHA256SUMS` | 20 | 27 |
  338: 
  339: 24 manifest files + `iolbox` + 2 payloads = 27. The double-stage byte-identity
  340: check and the deterministic tar flags are untouched.
  341: 
  342: ---
  343: 
  344: ## 6. `release-layout-test.sh` changes
  345: 
  346: - `--payload-arm64` / `--payload-arm64-sha256` added and required.
  347: - `expected-list.txt` gains the 6 assets and the arm64 payload (7 lines).
  348: - `SHA256SUMS` line count `20` ƒ+' `27`.
  349: - The `find ... -name 'iolbox-server-*.tar.gz' | wc -l` assertion changes from
  350:   `= 1` to `= 2`, **and** gains two explicit named checks: exactly one
  351:   `iolbox-server-<v>.tar.gz` and exactly one
  352:   `iolbox-server-<v>-linux-arm64.tar.gz`. A bare count of 2 would pass on two
  353:   arm64 payloads.
  354: - Both payloads' digests are checked against their trusted `--payload*-sha256`.
  355: - The reproducibility re-pack loop passes both pairs through to the packer.
  356: - The forbidden-member regex is unchanged. Verified by inspection that
  357:   `iolbox-server-<v>-linux-arm64.tar.gz` does not match any alternative in
  358:   `(\._[^/]*|\.DS_Store|.*namedfork.*|.*resourcefork.*|.*cisco.*|.*\.i86bi[^/]*|.*\.iol[^/]*)$`
  359:   ƒ?" in particular `.*\.iol[^/]*$` needs a literal `.iol`, and the archive path
  360:   contains `/iolbox`, not `.iolbox`.
  361: 
  362: **New assertion worth adding:** the arm64 payload's inner `manifest.env` says
  363: `arch=arm64`. The layout test should extract it and assert that, so a mislabelled
  364: payload is caught by the packaging gate rather than 40 minutes into a guest
  365: provision. Cheap, and it is the only in-archive proof that the second payload
  366: is genuinely arm64.
