docs\macos-m5-plan-review.md:5: The plan is **not safe to implement as written**. It does not need a wholesale rewrite, but 3-4, 6, 10, and 11 need the corrections below before implementation. The core environment-switch/conditional-`i386` design is sound and the normal launcher `start` path does rewrite an old canary drop-in, but the plan adds a speculative positive protocol field, treats optional server enforcement as if it were already-established semantics, derives execution mode from supervisor health, omits a real GUI Start surface, overstates the M4 machine's disposability, and describes a payload splice whose uncertainty has already been retired. With those points corrected, the plan is implementable. The complete UI file list is larger than the immutable M5 estimate suggests, but most changes within those files can be very small; cutting actual selection and Start surfaces would leave real honesty holes.
docs\macos-m5-plan-review.md:18: | 8 | **NOTE** | The feared "ordinary launcher start never rewrites an M1-M4 drop-in" hole does **not** exist in the current launcher. Both `start` and `upgrade` run the complete guest sequence, including `40-install-payload.sh`, which atomically rewrites the same drop-in. A mixed old-assets/new-payload invocation or raw `limactl start` can still run without the new environment until the M5 launcher provisions it. | CLI sends both commands to `runProvision` (`tools/iolab-launcher/macos_cli.go:153-202`). `runProvision` always stages files and calls `runGuestSequence` (`macos_lifecycle.go:349-354`); that sequence always runs `30-canary.sh`, `40-install-payload.sh`, and `50-verify.sh` (`macos_lifecycle.go:272-288`). The installer preserves drop-ins because it installs the base unit then starts it (`runtime/files/native/install.sh:250-291`); `40-install-payload.sh` rewrites the drop-in before invoking it (`packaging/macos/guest/40-install-payload.sh:197-213`). | Keep fail-open for non-Mac compatibility. Write `Environment=IOLBOX_DISABLE_I386=1` into the existing atomic drop-in, verify effective `Environment` plus correlated Hello in 40/50, and couple the M5 payload to M5 assets in staging/evidence. State that direct Lima starts or old asset bundles are outside the M5 launcher readiness contract; diagnostics must flag that drift rather than silently print healthy capability policy.
docs\macos-m5-plan-review.md:24: | 14 | **MAJOR** | 10's "must splice in the Linux guest" and open dry-run risk are obsolete. `runtime/pack-native.sh` cannot be used on this Windows/J: worktree, but the focused splice can be made on macOS. | The guest installer uses ordinary `tar -xzf`, then explicitly requires executable `install.sh` (`packaging/macos/guest/40-install-payload.sh:215-242`). The packer itself requires executable VPCS at preflight (`runtime/pack-native.sh:123-133`) and normally archives POSIX modes (`runtime/pack-native.sh:295-303`). `build-release.sh` already creates the GUI-embedded Linux/amd64 supervisor (`build-release.sh:18-33`). | Replace 10 with the completed path: build `supervisor/bin/supervisor-linux-amd64` through `build-release.sh` on Windows; scp it to the Mac; extract a copy of the known-good v0.5.3 tree; replace only `bin/supervisor`; `chmod 0755`; retar on macOS (prefer `COPYFILE_DISABLE=1` to avoid AppleDouble metadata). Record the existing probe `~/iolbox-m0/iolbox-server-v0.5.4-m5-probe.tar.gz`, sha256 `804aab32718d8f072bc2c38e135a6ec18a83e79c011a2df25c56754e0d0da022`. BSD tar's pax/ustar output is readable by GNU tar; proof is `tar -tzvf` mode inspection plus guest `tar -xzf`, `test -x .../install.sh .../bin/supervisor`, `bin/supervisor --version`, and a successful launcher upgrade. The splice does not need to occur inside the Linux guest.
docs\macos-m5-plan-review.md:45: 6. Keep fail-open, but make M5 asset/payload pairing explicit. Atomically add the environment line to the existing drop-in; verify effective `Environment`, `ExecStartPre`, correlated Hello omission, and readiness on both launcher `start` and `upgrade`.
