===== packaging/macos/README.release.md =====
    1: # iolbox on Apple Silicon macOS
    2: 
    3: This unsigned archive is the Apple Silicon (darwin/arm64) iolbox launcher,
    4: the exact Linux payload produced by the same release workflow, and the locked
    5: Lima profiles/provisioner it needs. It contains no guest disk, hypervisor,
    6: Lima installation, or Cisco software. You supply a legally held **x86_64 IOL**
    7: image; i386/i86bi and arm64-native IOL are unsupported.
    8: 
    9: ## Quick start
   10: 
   11: Prerequisites:
   12: 
   13: - Apple Silicon macOS with Rosetta available;
   14: - Lima installed and managed separately by you (Apache-2.0; it is not
   15:   redistributed or installed, upgraded, or removed by iolbox).
   16: 
   17: Download iolbox-macos-arm64.tar.gz and its matching
   18: iolbox-macos-arm64.tar.gz.sha256 from the same release. Verify the outer
   19: checksum before extracting:
   20: 
   21: ~~~sh
   22: shasum -a 256 -c iolbox-macos-arm64.tar.gz.sha256
   23: mkdir extract
   24: tar -xzf iolbox-macos-arm64.tar.gz -C extract
   25: cd extract/iolbox-macos-arm64
   26: shasum -a 256 -c SHA256SUMS
   27: ~~~
   28: 
   29: The artifact is unsigned. Before the first run, inspect quarantine on the
   30: downloaded archive, extracted directory, and launcher:
   31: 
   32: ~~~sh
   33: xattr -l /path/to/iolbox-macos-arm64.tar.gz || true
   34: xattr -p com.apple.quarantine /path/to/iolbox-macos-arm64.tar.gz; echo "archive xattr exit=$?"
   35: xattr -l .; echo "directory xattr exit=$?"
   36: xattr -p com.apple.quarantine ./iolbox; echo "binary xattr exit=$?"
   37: ~~~
   38: 
   39: If the launcher has com.apple.quarantine, remove that attribute from the
   40: launcher only, after the checksum checks above:
   41: 
   42: ~~~sh
   43: xattr -d com.apple.quarantine ./iolbox
   44: ~~~
   45: 
   46: Then start the product from the extracted directory with one command:
   47: 
   48: ~~~sh
   49: ./iolbox start
   50: ~~~
   51: 
   52: The launcher creates or reuses the durable named Lima guest
   53: iolbox-debian13, verifies the VZ/Rosetta canary and pinned Debian 13/trixie
   54: guest/kernel (6.12.101+deb13-cloud-arm64), and serves the GUI on
   55: http://127.0.0.1:4001. The GUI, console, and capture forwards are
   56: loopback-only and have no authentication.
   57: 
   58: For the compatibility profile, select jammy; it is pinned to Ubuntu 22.04
   59: with the qualified 5.15 guest/kernel line. The Debian 12/bookworm files are
   60: shipped as an unqualified candidate and are refused while their digest remains
   61: unpinned.
   62: 
   63: Host data defaults to:
   64: 
   65: ~~~text
   66: ~/Library/Application Support/iolbox/images
   67: ~/Library/Application Support/iolbox/labs
   68: ~~~
   69: 
   70: ./iolbox stop stops the guest and preserves its VM, images, labs, license,
   71: and Lima cache. Removing the extracted launcher directory is ordinary,
   72: non-destructive uninstall. An optional destructive reset may delete only the
   73: named iolbox-debian13 Lima machine and move the two iolbox data paths to
   74: Trash after explicit checks; it is not part of ordinary launcher removal.
   75: 
   76: ## Upgrade and support boundary
   77: 
   78: From a later extracted release, run ./iolbox upgrade in the existing data
   79: environment. It preserves host images/labs, the host identity and license,
   80: the Lima machine, and the pinned guest kernel while replacing the guest
   81: payload.
   82: 
   83: The runtime executes the existing Linux supervisor/VPCS and x86_64 IOL under
   84: Rosetta inside the arm64 Lima guest. This release supports **x86_64 IOL only**;
   85: i386/i86bi and arm64-native IOL are not supported and are not made runnable by
   86: the archive.
   87: 
   88: The installation guide for this exact release is:
   89: <https://github.com/rohan-punj/iolbox/blob/@VERSION@/docs/INSTALL.md>
   90: 
   91: The archive's SHA-256 files detect corruption or a changed download. Because
   92: this release is unsigned, they do not authenticate the publisher. Lima fetches
   93: the digest-locked Debian/Ubuntu guest image and apt packages at provisioning
   94: time; those guest assets are not embedded here. See notices/THIRD_PARTY.md
   95: for the distributed VPCS notice and the boundary around Windows-only QEMU.
===== THIRD_PARTY.md =====
    1: # Third-party software distributed with iolbox
    2: 
    3: iolbox bundles the following third-party components. Their licenses are their
    4: own; this file records what we ship, from where, pinned to an exact version and
    5: checksum, and how we comply with each license.
    6: 
    7: ---
    8: 
    9: ## Apple Silicon macOS archive
   10: 
   11: The `iolbox-macos-arm64.tar.gz` archive contains the Go `iolbox` launcher, the
   12: exact native Linux payload produced by `runtime/pack-native.sh`, the locked
   13: Lima profile/provisioner files, and this notice. It contains no guest disk,
   14: hypervisor, Lima installation, or Cisco software.
   15: 
   16: ### Separately installed and fetched components
   17: 
   18: - Lima is an Apache-2.0 licensed, user-installed prerequisite. iolbox neither
   19:   redistributes nor installs, upgrades, or removes Lima.
   20: - Lima downloads the digest-locked Debian 13/trixie default or Ubuntu
   21:   22.04/Jammy compatibility guest image. The guest image, apt package indexes,
   22:   and guest packages are fetched at provisioning time and are not embedded in
   23:   the archive.
   24: - Users supply any legally held IOL/IOU image. iolbox does not distribute or
   25:   depend on Cisco software; x86_64 IOL is the only supported IOL architecture
   26:   in this Rosetta profile. i386/i86bi and arm64-native IOL are unsupported.
   27: 
   28: ### VPCS binary redistributed inside the native payload
   29: 
   30: `runtime/fetch-vpcs.sh` checks out the GNS3 VPCS project at the immutable
   31: `v0.8.3` ref (release commit `3870ae8`) and builds its Linux/amd64 binary;
   32: `runtime/pack-native.sh` places that binary at `bin/vpcs` in the native
   33: payload. The upstream project is BSD-2-Clause licensed:
   34: 
   35: - Source repository: <https://github.com/GNS3/vpcs>
   36: - Pinned source archive: <https://github.com/GNS3/vpcs/archive/refs/tags/v0.8.3.tar.gz>
   37: - Pinned release: <https://github.com/GNS3/vpcs/releases/tag/v0.8.3>
   38: - License: BSD-2-Clause, as identified by the upstream repository
   39: 
   40: The archive redistributes the resulting binary, not a moving branch checkout.
   41: The source URL and ref above are the corresponding source offer for this
   42: version; the build recipe and compiler/link flags are recorded in
   43: `runtime/fetch-vpcs.sh`.
   44: 
   45: QEMU is **not** in the Apple Silicon archive. The QEMU notice below applies
   46: only to the separate Windows launcher bundle and must not be read as a claim
   47: that the Mac artifact contains QEMU.
   48: 
   49: ---
   50: 
   51: ## QEMU (Windows build) ƒ?" the `qemu` compatibility backend
   52: 
   53: The Windows launcher (`tools/iolab-launcher`) ships a trimmed copy of QEMU so the
   54: `qemu` backend (software-emulated TCG, see `docs/providers.md` and
   55: `runtime/qemu-compat.md`) works on any Windows machine with zero prerequisites.
   56: 
   57: ### Pinned source
   58: 
   59: | Field | Value |
   60: |---|---|
   61: | Upstream | Stefan Weil's official QEMU-for-Windows builds ƒ?" <https://qemu.weilnetz.de/w64/> |
   62: | File | `qemu-w64-setup-20260501.exe` |
   63: | Full URL | <https://qemu.weilnetz.de/w64/qemu-w64-setup-20260501.exe> |
   64: | QEMU version | 11.0.0 release build (reports `11.0.50 (v11.0.0-12631-g54e84cdc7a)`) |
   65: | Installer size | 190 MB (198,760,632 bytes) |
   66: | **SHA-256 (installer, verified by download)** | `a8b29572afb4c6ad024b7de129c81033e9fd191b9e054e3a52ea0bed24ac19ef` |
   67: 
   68: `qemu.weilnetz.de` is the canonical upstream-linked Windows build (referenced
   69: from qemu.org's download page). Note: recent installers are Authenticode-signed
   70: with an **expired certificate** ƒ?" Windows SmartScreen may warn on the installer.
   71: This does not affect the extracted binaries we redistribute (we extract, we do
   72: not run the installer on user machines).
   73: 
   74: ### How the redistributable is produced (extraction, not install)
   75: 
   76: The upstream `.exe` is an NSIS installer. We do **not** run it on user machines;
   77: we extract it once and ship a trimmed tree:
   78: 
   79: ```sh
   80: # 1. verify the pinned installer
   81: sha256sum qemu-w64-setup-20260501.exe
   82: #   -> a8b29572afb4c6ad024b7de129c81033e9fd191b9e054e3a52ea0bed24ac19ef
   83: 
   84: # 2. extract with 7-Zip (NSIS archive)
   85: "C:\Program Files\7-Zip\7z.exe" x -oqemu-extract qemu-w64-setup-20260501.exe
   86: 
   87: # 3. trim to the headless x86_64 target:
   88: #    - keep  qemu-system-x86_64.exe  + ALL *.dll (the runtime DLL set)
   89: #    - keep  share/keymaps/  and the x86/i386 firmware blobs
   90: #            (bios*.bin, vgabios*.bin, kvmvapic.bin, linuxboot*/multiboot*/pvh,
   91: #             *-virtio.rom + pxe-*.rom, edk2-i386/x86_64 *.fd)
   92: #    - drop  the other 55 qemu-system-*.exe targets, share/doc, and the
   93: #            non-x86 firmware (openbios-*, edk2-aarch64/arm, dtb, ...)
   94: ```
   95: 
   96: Result: a `qemu/` directory of ~175 MB. Verified standalone on a clean box:
   97: `qemu-system-x86_64.exe -version` and `-accel help` (lists `tcg` + `whpx`) both
   98: run, so the DLL set is complete.
   99: 
  100: ### Layout shipped next to the launcher exe
  101: 
  102: ```
  103: iolbox-launcher.exe
  104: iolbox-disk.qcow2            (the runtime disk ƒ?" a separate release asset, see below)
  105: qemu/
  106:   qemu-system-x86_64.exe
  107:   *.dll                     (the runtime DLLs)
  108:   share/
  109:     keymaps/
  110:     bios*.bin, vgabios*.bin, kvmvapic.bin, linuxboot*.bin, ...
  111: ```
  112: 
  113: The launcher looks for `qemu/qemu-system-x86_64.exe` and `iolbox-disk.qcow2`
  114: relative to its own exe (`--qemu` / `--disk` override for dev). QEMU resolves its
  115: BIOS/keymap data from the `share/` dir next to its exe automatically.
  116: 
  117: The `qemu/` directory and `*.qcow2` are **git-ignored** (see
  118: `tools/iolab-launcher/.gitignore`) ƒ?" they are release-time assets staged into the
  119: bundle, never committed to the repo.
  120: 
  121: ### License / GPL compliance
  122: 
  123: QEMU is licensed under the **GNU GPL v2** (with some components under LGPL v2.1
  124: and other compatible licenses). We redistribute unmodified upstream binaries.
  125: 
  126: - The upstream binaries carry their license texts (`COPYING`, `COPYING.LIB`) in
  127:   the installer; include a copy of `COPYING`/`COPYING.LIB` in the shipped `qemu/`
  128:   directory (or link to them from the release notes) when assembling a release.
  129: - **Written offer for source:** the corresponding source for this exact build is
  130:   published by the upstream packager at the QEMU source page,
  131:   <https://www.qemu.org/download/#source> (release 11.0.0), mirrored from
  132:   <https://qemu.weilnetz.de/>. iolbox distributes QEMU **unmodified**; the source
  133:   offer therefore points at upstream. Include this URL in the release notes /
  134:   About page alongside the version above so the offer travels with the binary.
  135: 
  136: ### The iolbox runtime disk (`iolbox-disk.qcow2`)
  137: 
  138: Not third-party ƒ?" it is built from `runtime/` (Debian-slim rootfs + the iolbox
  139: supervisor) by `runtime/pack-qemu.sh`. It contains **no Cisco software**; IOL
  140: images are supplied by the user at runtime via the GUI. Debian's own components
  141: inside the rootfs are under their respective licenses (Debian is DFSG-free);
  142: this is the same rootfs the WSL2 and VMware artifacts ship, already covered by
  143: the runtime build.

